(window.TPR_CH = window.TPR_CH || []).push(
{"id":"c-p-01","n":1001,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Planning a pregnancy on sertraline: weigh the risks, start folic acid, write the plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:10","where":"Outpatient clinic, community mental health team","text":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent depression has been well for a year on sertraline 100 mg. Her last episode, in 2023, was severe, with suicidal thoughts. She and her husband want to start trying for a baby in three months. She says, 'I've read SSRIs harm babies. Should I just stop now?'"},"task":"Review her mental health and relapse risk, explain the risks and benefits of continuing sertraline before and during pregnancy, start folic acid, and agree a written plan she owns.","first60":["Read the notes: number and severity of episodes, any self-harm, and what happened the last time sertraline stopped.","Check she has not already stopped or cut the dose, and whether she could be pregnant now.","Check other medicines, supplements, alcohol, smoking, BMI and any reason she needs folic acid 5 mg.","Have the bumps (UKTIS) sertraline leaflet and the NHS folic acid page ready to hand over.","Plan a shared decision: her relapse risk, the drug facts and what matters to her, all written down."],"ask":["'What have you read or been told about sertraline in pregnancy?'","'How are you now: mood, sleep, enjoyment, any dark thoughts?'","'What was the worst point of your last episode, and how fast did it come on?'","'Have you ever stopped sertraline before? What happened?'","'Could you be pregnant now? When was your last period, and what contraception are you using?'","'Are you taking folic acid, or any other medicines, supplements or herbal remedies?'","'Do you drink, smoke or use anything else?'","'Has anyone in your family been unwell during pregnancy or after a birth?'"],"recognise":["Two episodes, one severe, and relapse within months of stopping before: her risk of relapse off sertraline is high.","Depression in pregnancy is linked with poor self-care, missed antenatal care, preterm birth, low birthweight and postnatal depression.","Sertraline is one of the best-studied antidepressants in pregnancy and is not linked with a clear rise in major birth defects.","Late-pregnancy exposure: some babies are jittery, sleepy or feed poorly for a few days (poor neonatal adaptation); there is a small rise in the rare risk of PPHN.","SSRIs in the month before birth carry a small increased risk of postpartum haemorrhage (MHRA 2021).","Stopping suddenly on a positive test is common and risky: discontinuation symptoms, then relapse."],"decide":[{"c":"Continue sertraline 100 mg, start folic acid 400 micrograms daily now, record her informed decision with a relapse plan, write to the GP and ask the perinatal team for preconception advice.","r":"Right: her relapse risk is high, sertraline is a well-studied choice, and the decision is informed, shared and written down.","best":true},{"c":"Advise her to stop sertraline now so she is medicine-free before she conceives.","r":"Unsafe: she relapsed within three months of stopping before and her last episode was severe. If, after hearing the risks, she still wants to try, taper slowly with a relapse plan.","best":false},{"c":"Switch her to a different antidepressant that is 'safer in pregnancy'.","r":"Wrong: sertraline is already among the best-studied options. Switching risks relapse and exposes the baby to two medicines.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Decision making and consent (2020): explain the material risks and benefits of each option, including stopping and doing nothing, and record her choice.","NICE CG192: discuss the risks of untreated illness and of treatment with any woman with a mental health problem who is planning a pregnancy, taking account of her history and preferences.","MHRA Drug Safety Update (2021): SSRI or SNRI use in the month before delivery is linked with a small increased risk of postpartum haemorrhage.","Confidentiality: share the plan with the GP, and with her consent with the midwife at booking."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"It makes complete sense to worry about the baby. The honest picture is that sertraline is one of the best-studied medicines in pregnancy, and becoming unwell again carries risks too. Let's weigh it up together."},{"to":"Patient","s":"If your test is positive, please don't stop the sertraline. Keep taking it, book with the midwife and ring us; we'll review you within two weeks."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Plan agreed: continue sertraline 100 mg through conception and pregnancy; folic acid 400 micrograms daily started. Please flag SSRI use at booking so the maternity team can watch the baby after birth and plan for bleeding risk."}],"write":"Clinic review. 32F, recurrent depressive disorder, currently in remission 12 months on sertraline 100 mg.\nPlanning pregnancy in ~3 months. LMP 2/52 ago, on COC. Not on folic acid. BMI 24. Alcohol 4 units/week, non-smoker. Mother had postnatal depression.\nHistory: 2 episodes; 2023 severe with suicidal ideation, no attempt. Stopped sertraline 2022 and relapsed within 3 months.\nMSE: euthymic, no suicidal ideation.\nDiscussed: risks of relapse and untreated depression; sertraline not linked to major malformations; neonatal adaptation; PPHN about 3 in 1000 vs 2 in 1000; small PPH risk; compatible with breastfeeding. Teach-back correct.\nPlan: she chooses to continue sertraline 100 mg. Folic acid 400 micrograms daily now to 12 weeks. Not to stop if pregnant: contact GP, midwife and CMHT. GP letter. Perinatal team advice requested. Review 3 months or sooner if pregnant.\nDr S. Adeyemi CT2","make":["Weigh three things aloud: her relapse risk, what is known about sertraline, and what matters most to her.","Use absolute numbers: PPHN rises from about 2 to about 3 in 1000 babies; 997 in 1000 are unaffected.","Folic acid 400 micrograms daily from now until 12 weeks; 5 mg if BMI 30 or more, diabetes, antiepileptic or HIV medicines, sickle cell or thalassaemia, or a personal or family history of neural tube defect.","Write her relapse signature and the 'if pregnant' steps into the letter, copied to her.","Her last episode was severe: ask the perinatal mental health team for preconception advice or assessment.","Give the bumps sertraline leaflet so she can share it with her husband."],"next":"She decides to stay on sertraline 100 mg and starts folic acid that day. Four months later she rings: her test is positive. She has kept taking sertraline, has booked with the midwife, and the perinatal team will see her at 12 weeks.","marks":["Asked what she had read before giving facts.","Established relapse risk, including what happened when she last stopped.","Gave the main risks in absolute numbers, without false reassurance.","Started folic acid at the right dose.","Told her not to stop sertraline suddenly if she becomes pregnant.","Recorded her decision and copied the plan to the GP."],"pitfalls":["Telling her to stop sertraline 'to be safe'.","Saying it is 'completely safe' instead of giving honest numbers.","Forgetting folic acid, or the 5 mg dose for those who need it.","Switching to another antidepressant just because of pregnancy."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. UKTIS. bumps: Use of sertraline in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA Drug Safety Update: SSRI/SNRI use during pregnancy and risk of postpartum haemorrhage (January 2021; checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Vitamins, supplements and nutrition in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK women refer themselves to a midwife for booking, ideally by 10 weeks. Copying clinic letters to the patient is normal practice, and bumps leaflets are the standard patient source on medicines in pregnancy.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Medication review + Explain a treatment","do":"Weigh continuing sertraline against relapse with her, start folic acid, and record a shared preconception plan.","obj":["Established her relapse risk: number and severity of episodes and what happened when she last stopped sertraline","Explained the risks of sertraline in pregnancy in absolute numbers, alongside the risks of relapse","Started folic acid 400 micrograms daily and checked whether she needs 5 mg","Agreed what she will do if she becomes pregnant, including not stopping sertraline suddenly","Recorded her decision and arranged a GP letter, perinatal advice and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've read SSRIs cause heart defects. I'd never forgive myself.\"","r":"\"That worry shows how much you care already. Some early studies raised that question, but larger, better studies of sertraline haven't shown a clear rise in birth defects. Let me give you the real numbers.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just stop for the pregnancy and go back on after?\"","r":"\"I can see why that's tempting. Last time you stopped, you were unwell within three months, and pregnancy and the newborn months are hard times to relapse. If you want to try, we'd taper slowly with a clear plan.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the baby be born addicted?\"","r":"\"No. Some babies are a bit jittery, sleepy or slow to feed for the first few days, and it settles on its own. The midwives just keep a closer eye after birth.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"What kind of mother takes pills while pregnant?\"","r":"\"A mother who's looking after her health so she can look after her baby. Treating depression is part of caring for the pregnancy, just like treating asthma or diabetes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I breastfeed on it?\"","r":"\"Yes. Very little gets into breast milk, and sertraline is one of the antidepressants we prefer when women breastfeed.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My mum had a breakdown after she had me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that matters. It raises your chance of becoming unwell after a birth, so I'd like the perinatal team involved early to plan with you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue sertraline 100 mg once daily (morning); do not stop abruptly; keep the lowest dose that keeps her well, reviewed each trimester","Folic acid 400 micrograms daily from now until 12 weeks of pregnancy; 5 mg only if a high-risk group applies","Vitamin D 10 micrograms daily in pregnancy and while breastfeeding (NHS advice)","Advise no alcohol when trying to conceive or in pregnancy, as the safest approach"],"psych":["Written relapse signature agreed today: early waking, withdrawing from friends, losing interest","If low mood returns in pregnancy: fast-track CBT through NHS Talking Therapies, which prioritise perinatal women"],"social":["Invite her husband to the next review; give him the bumps leaflet and the warning signs","GP to flag the plan; midwife told at booking so maternity can plan care after birth"],"safety":["If pregnant: keep taking sertraline, contact GP and midwife, CMHT review within 2 weeks","Crisis: CMHT duty line in hours, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if in danger","Perinatal team advice requested now because her last episode was severe; review in 3 months"]},"facts":[{"h":"Don't stop on a positive test","x":"Stopping sertraline suddenly brings discontinuation symptoms and relapse. Tell every woman of childbearing age on an antidepressant: if pregnant, keep taking it and ring us."},{"h":"PPHN in numbers","x":"Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn: about 2 in 1000 babies normally, about 3 in 1000 after late-pregnancy SSRI exposure. Give absolute numbers, never 'doubles the risk'."},{"h":"Bleeding risk before birth","x":"MHRA 2021: SSRIs in the month before delivery slightly raise postpartum haemorrhage risk. The maternity team needs to know; it is not a reason to stop."},{"h":"Who needs folic acid 5 mg","x":"BMI 30 or more, diabetes, antiepileptic or HIV medicines, sickle cell or thalassaemia, or a personal or family history of neural tube defect. Everyone else: 400 micrograms."},{"h":"Don't switch for pregnancy","x":"If a medicine works, switching to another 'safer' one risks relapse and exposes the baby to two drugs. NICE advises weighing this before any change."}],"sim":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, community mental health clinic. Mrs Priya Shah, 32, has recurrent depression and has been well for a year on sertraline 100 mg. She wants to try for a baby. Review her, explain the options and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Priya Shah, 32, female, patient","open":"Thanks for seeing me. I've been reading online and I'm scared. I think I should come off the sertraline before we start trying.","manner":"Anxious and well-read; asks pointed questions. Tearful when recalling 2023. Relaxes when given honest numbers and when her choice is respected.","free":["Well for over a year: working, sleeping, enjoying things","Read that SSRIs cause heart defects and newborn withdrawal","She and her husband Dev want to start trying in three months","Wants to do everything right for the baby"],"ifasked":["Stopped sertraline in 2022 after six months and relapsed within three months","2023 episode: off work four months, thought Dev would be better off without her, no attempt","No suicidal thoughts for over a year","Not taking folic acid or any other medicine","Last period two weeks ago; on the combined pill","Drinks two glasses of wine at weekends; never smoked","BMI 24 at the GP last month; no diabetes or epilepsy","Her mother had postnatal depression"],"worries":["Will the baby be born 'addicted'?","Will sertraline cause a birth defect?","Can she breastfeed on it?","What if she gets pregnant before the next appointment?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful talking about 2023; settles if validated","If told sertraline is 'completely safe', asks why the internet says otherwise","Relaxes noticeably once given absolute numbers","Wants to talk to Dev before a final decision; accepts if that is respected"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms purpose and asks what she has read or fears","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her worry about the baby before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks current mood and asks directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Establishes episode history, severity and outcome of stopping sertraline before","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks pregnancy status, contraception, other medicines, alcohol, smoking and folic acid risk","d":"Content"},{"c":"States sertraline is not linked to a clear rise in birth defects and suits breastfeeding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains neonatal adaptation, PPHN and bleeding risk in plain words with absolute numbers","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains risks of relapse and untreated depression for mother and baby","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises folic acid 400 micrograms daily now until 12 weeks, with reason for that dose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises not stopping suddenly if pregnant and gives warning signs and crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and respects her right to decide","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises the plan, GP letter, perinatal advice, leaflet and review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Shah, I'm Dr Adeyemi, one of the psychiatry doctors. I understand you'd like to talk about pregnancy. What's on your mind?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me. I've been reading online and I'm scared. I think I should come off the sertraline before we start trying."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That's a really natural worry. Wanting to protect a baby before it even exists says a lot about you. What have you read?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"That it causes heart defects. And babies come out withdrawing, like they're addicted. I couldn't live with that."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. I'll go through what's actually known, honestly, with real numbers. First, can I check how you are at the moment?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Honestly, good. The best I've been in years. Working, sleeping, enjoying things."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. Any low days lately, or felt that life isn't worth living?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"No. Not for over a year."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Her history","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me about the times you've been unwell before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Twice. 2022 wasn't too bad. 2023 was awful. I couldn't get out of bed. I thought Dev would be better off without me."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a really dark time, and I'm sorry you went through it. Thank you for telling me. Did you ever act on those thoughts?","m":[2,3,4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"No. I was off work four months. The sertraline and the therapy got me out of it."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"Have you ever stopped the sertraline before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"In 2022. I felt better after six months, so I stopped. Three months later I was low again."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"That's important. It tells us your depression can come back fairly quickly without it.","m":[4,8]},{"t":"1:58","band":"Health now","w":"D","s":"Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"No. My period was two weeks ago and I'm on the pill until we start trying."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Are you taking any other medicines, supplements or herbal remedies? Any folic acid?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Should I be?"},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Yes, we'll come to that. Do you drink or smoke?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"A couple of glasses of wine at weekends. Never smoked."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Any diabetes or epilepsy, and do you know your weight or BMI?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"No to both. The GP said my BMI was 24 last month."},{"t":"2:42","band":"The facts","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So here's the honest picture. Sertraline is one of the most studied antidepressants in pregnancy. Large studies haven't shown a clear rise in major birth defects, including heart defects.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"But the article said there was a link."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Some early studies raised it. Bigger studies that allowed for the depression itself didn't confirm it. No medicine is proven completely safe, but this is reassuring.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"And the withdrawal thing?"},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"If you take it late in pregnancy, some babies are jittery, sleepy or slow to feed for the first few days. It's usually mild and settles on its own. The midwives just watch the baby more closely after birth.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"OK. That's less scary than addicted."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"There's also a rare lung condition in newborns called PPHN. Normally about 2 in 1000 babies get it. With sertraline late in pregnancy it's about 3 in 1000. So 997 in 1000 don't.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Right. That's not what I imagined."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"And taking it in the month before birth slightly raises the chance of heavier bleeding after delivery. The maternity team just need to know so they're prepared.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"So why not stop and be completely safe?"},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Stopping isn't risk-free either. Last time you were unwell within three months. Depression in pregnancy can affect eating, sleep and antenatal care, is linked with early birth and smaller babies, and makes depression after birth more likely.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"I don't want to be like 2023 with a newborn."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"No. You've worked hard to get well, and I want to protect that as well as the baby.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Can I breastfeed on it?"},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Yes. Very little gets into breast milk, and it's one of the antidepressants we prefer for breastfeeding.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:47","band":"The plan","w":"D","s":"Folic acid now: 400 micrograms every day, from today until you're 12 weeks pregnant. Any pharmacy sells it. With your BMI and health, you don't need the higher 5 milligram dose.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I'll get some today."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"And if your period's late or a test is positive, please don't stop sertraline suddenly. Keep taking it, book with the midwife, tell your GP, and ring us so we can review you.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"What if I start feeling low in the pregnancy?"},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Tell us early: sleep changing, losing interest, pulling away from people. Ring your GP or us; out of hours, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option. If you ever feel unsafe, call 999 or go to A&E.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, could you tell me in your words what we've covered?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"No clear link with defects. The baby might be jittery at first. Small risks with the lungs and bleeding. Stopping could make me ill again. Folic acid now."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. What are you leaning towards?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"Staying on it. But I want to talk to Dev first."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"That's sensible. It's your decision, and it's fine to take time. Here's a leaflet on sertraline in pregnancy to share with him.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:17","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: sertraline 100 milligrams for now, folic acid from today, and no sudden stopping if you get pregnant. I'll write to your GP with a copy to you, ask the perinatal team for advice because 2023 was severe, and see you in three months, sooner if you're pregnant.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel so much better about it."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Any other questions before you go?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. That's everything."}],"global":{"pass":"Validates her fear, establishes high relapse risk, gives honest absolute risks, starts folic acid and records her informed choice with a plan for a positive test.","borderline":"Accurate facts but thin relapse history, or little check of understanding and no written follow-up.","fail":"Advises stopping sertraline or calls it 'completely safe', omits folic acid, or gives no plan if she becomes pregnant."}}},
{"id":"c-p-02","n":1002,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Bipolar disorder at 12 weeks: the perinatal plan, the birth plan and the postpartum risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:30","where":"Antenatal clinic, maternity unit (joint clinic with the specialist perinatal mental health team)","text":"The midwife flagged a 30-year-old woman at her 12-week booking and scan. She has bipolar I disorder, with one manic admission in 2021, and has been well for three years on quetiapine 300 mg at night. This is her first baby. Her partner, 33, is with her. She asks, 'Is it true I could get ill after the birth?'"},"task":"Review her mental health and medication, explain her postpartum relapse risk honestly, and start the perinatal mental health plan, birth plan and referrals.","first60":["Read the booking notes and GP summary: diagnosis, admissions, psychotic symptoms, current dose, adherence.","Check she is still taking quetiapine every night; many women stop on a positive test.","Look for a family history of bipolar disorder or postpartum psychosis: it raises her risk further.","Note BMI and booking bloods: antipsychotics raise the risk of diabetes in pregnancy.","Know the team you will copy in: perinatal mental health team, named midwife, obstetrician, GP, health visitor."],"ask":["'How have you been since you found out you were pregnant: mood, sleep, energy?'","'Are you still taking the quetiapine every night? Any missed or reduced doses?'","'When you became unwell in 2021, what were the very first signs?'","'Did you have unusual beliefs or hear things then?'","'Has anyone in your family had bipolar disorder or become unwell after having a baby?'","'Who will be at home with you after the birth, and who could do a night feed?'","'Have you thought about how you'd like to feed the baby?'","To her partner: 'What would you notice first if she was becoming unwell?'"],"recognise":["Bipolar disorder carries about a 1 in 5 risk of postpartum psychosis, mostly in the first 2 weeks after birth; risk rises if she stops medication or has a family history.","Stopping an antipsychotic or mood stabiliser in pregnancy sharply raises relapse risk; the benefit to the baby is small.","Quetiapine is not linked with a clear rise in major birth defects; it does raise weight and the risk of diabetes in pregnancy.","Babies exposed to antipsychotics late in pregnancy may have temporary withdrawal or movement symptoms after birth (MHRA).","Sleep loss around labour and the first nights is a strong trigger for mania and psychosis.","Valproate is contraindicated in pregnancy for bipolar disorder; lithium only with specialist planning and level monitoring."],"decide":[{"c":"Continue quetiapine 300 mg, refer to the specialist perinatal team today, plan glucose screening, and build a written perinatal plan (including birth and postnatal care) to be shared by 32 weeks.","r":"Right: her risk is high and predictable. Continuing treatment and planning early, with everyone holding the same plan, is what prevents a crisis.","best":true},{"c":"Stop quetiapine now that she is past the first trimester, to protect the baby.","r":"Unsafe: relapse risk rises sharply off treatment and an episode in pregnancy or after birth is dangerous for both.","best":false},{"c":"Continue quetiapine and leave planning until after the birth.","r":"Too late: the highest risk is the first 2 weeks after birth. The plan must be written, agreed and shared before labour.","best":false}],"law":["NICE CG192: refer any woman who has, or has had, a severe mental illness to a specialist perinatal mental health service in pregnancy, and write an integrated care plan shared with her, maternity, GP and health visitor.","MHRA (2024): valproate must not be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists independently document that nothing else is suitable; it is contraindicated in pregnancy for bipolar disorder.","MCA 2005: she can write an advance statement now about treatment and the baby's care if she becomes unwell; it is not binding but must be taken into account.","A diagnosis of bipolar disorder alone is not a reason for a child protection referral; refer only for a specific risk to the baby."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Yes, it's true that the weeks after birth are a higher-risk time. About 1 in 5 women with bipolar disorder become seriously unwell. The good news is we know this now, and planning really does help."},{"to":"Partner","s":"You know her best. If she's sleeping much less, talking fast or saying unusual things, ring the perinatal team that same day, not next week."},{"to":"Midwife","s":"Please flag her as high risk for postpartum psychosis, book a glucose tolerance test at 24 to 28 weeks, and make sure the written perinatal plan is in her notes and on labour ward by 32 weeks."}],"write":"Joint antenatal clinic. 30F, G1, 12+2 weeks. Bipolar I disorder; 1 admission (mania with psychotic features) 2021. Well 3 years on quetiapine 300 mg nocte, adherent.\nNo current mood or psychotic symptoms; sleep 7 h; no suicidal ideation. Early signs 2021: sleeping 3 h, spending, irritability. FH: maternal aunt bipolar. BMI 27.\nDiscussed: postpartum psychosis risk about 1 in 5, highest first 2 weeks; quetiapine not linked to major malformations; diabetes risk; possible neonatal withdrawal signs; breastfeeding generally compatible.\nPlan: continue quetiapine 300 mg. Referral to specialist perinatal team (accepted today). OGTT 24-28 weeks; weight and BP each visit. Perinatal plan drafted; to be finalised and shared by 32 weeks. Partner given warning signs and contacts. Review 20 weeks.\nDr M. Osei CT3","make":["Draft the plan headings now: relapse signature, medicines in pregnancy and after birth, birth plan, feeding plan, postnatal stay, contacts in the first 2 weeks, crisis numbers, MBU if needed.","Birth plan: early effective pain relief, a single room after birth, partner allowed to stay, a protected block of night sleep with the partner doing one feed.","Postnatal: perinatal team to see her before discharge and regularly in the first 2 weeks; no early discharge without their agreement.","Monitoring: weight and BP each visit, glucose tolerance test at 24 to 28 weeks.","Tell the paediatric team in the third trimester so the baby is observed for withdrawal or movement signs after birth.","Breastfeeding on quetiapine is generally compatible; plan who covers night feeds so she can sleep."],"next":"She stays on quetiapine. At 32 weeks the perinatal plan is signed off and uploaded to the maternity record. She gives birth at 39 weeks, sleeps in a side room with her partner doing the 2 am feed, and is seen by the perinatal team on day 2 and weekly after.","marks":["Checked adherence and early warning signs from 2021.","Gave the postpartum risk as about 1 in 5, highest in the first 2 weeks.","Advised continuing quetiapine and explained why.","Planned glucose screening and newborn observation.","Described a written plan shared with all teams by 32 weeks.","Gave her partner warning signs and same-day contacts."],"pitfalls":["Advising her to stop quetiapine.","Leaving the plan until the third trimester ends or after the birth.","Forgetting sleep protection in the birth plan.","Making a safeguarding referral on the diagnosis alone."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Postpartum psychosis: information resource (checked Nov 2026)","4. MHRA Drug Safety Update: valproate use in men and women under 55 (2024; checked Nov 2026)","5. UKTIS. bumps: Use of quetiapine in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: Mother and Baby Units are regional NHS inpatient units where mother and baby are admitted together; know which one serves your trust. In the UK the 'perinatal plan' is a real document the labour ward expects to find in the notes.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Management plan","do":"Explain her postpartum relapse risk, keep quetiapine going, and start a written perinatal and birth plan with referrals.","obj":["Confirmed quetiapine adherence, her early warning signs and family history","Explained her postpartum psychosis risk as about 1 in 5, highest in the first 2 weeks","Agreed to continue quetiapine 300 mg and arranged glucose screening at 24 to 28 weeks","Set out a birth and postnatal plan that protects sleep, with named contacts in the first 2 weeks","Referred her to the specialist perinatal team and agreed who receives the written plan by 32 weeks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you saying I'm going to go mad after the birth?\"","r":"\"No. Most women with bipolar disorder stay well after birth. About 1 in 5 become unwell, and knowing that early lets us plan. Planning is the best protection there is.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I want to come off the quetiapine. I don't want her on drugs.\"","r":"\"I understand; it's a protective instinct. Quetiapine hasn't been linked with a clear rise in birth defects, and stopping makes relapse much more likely, which carries real risks for you both. Can we go through it?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will they take the baby off me if I get ill?\"","r":"\"Having bipolar disorder doesn't mean that. If you did need hospital, there are Mother and Baby Units where you and your baby stay together. The plan we're writing is about keeping you together and well.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Partner) \"What do I actually look for?\"","r":"\"Her own early signs: sleeping much less but not tired, talking fast, irritability, spending, or saying things that don't make sense. If you see them, ring the perinatal team that day.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why would I need a single room? I want to be on the ward like everyone else.\"","r":"\"That's fair, and it's your choice. Sleep is the biggest trigger for you, and open bays are noisy at night. A side room with your partner helps you get a proper block of sleep.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I was in hospital for six weeks last time. I can't go through that again with a baby.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a really hard time, and it makes sense you're scared. This time we know your risk in advance, and we'll have a plan ready long before the baby arrives.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue quetiapine 300 mg at night; no dose change unless symptoms emerge; review each trimester with the perinatal psychiatrist","Weight and BP at every antenatal visit; oral glucose tolerance test at 24 to 28 weeks","Vitamin D 10 micrograms daily; folic acid 400 micrograms until 12 weeks","Agree in advance, in the plan, what the perinatal psychiatrist will offer if sleep fails after birth","Paediatric team told in the third trimester; baby observed after birth for withdrawal or movement signs"],"psych":["Written relapse signature with her and her partner: sleep under 5 hours, racing thoughts, spending, irritability","Perinatal team community nurse as her named contact through pregnancy and the first year"],"social":["Partner to take paternity leave for the first 2 weeks and do one night feed (expressed milk or formula)","Health visitor antenatal contact from 28 weeks, copied into the plan","Mother or another named relative to stay for the first fortnight if possible"],"safety":["Written perinatal plan by 32 weeks, shared with her, midwife, obstetrician, labour ward, GP and health visitor","Crisis contacts in the plan: perinatal team in hours, crisis team or NHS 111 mental health option out of hours; nearest MBU named","Reviews at 20, 28 and 32 to 36 weeks; seen before discharge after birth and regularly for the first 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"About 1 in 5 after birth","x":"Women with bipolar disorder have about a 1 in 5 risk of postpartum psychosis; about 1 in 2 after a previous episode. Most cases start within 2 weeks of birth."},{"h":"Plan written by 32 weeks","x":"The perinatal mental health plan must be in the maternity notes before labour: early signs, medicines, birth plan, feeding, postnatal contacts and crisis numbers."},{"h":"Sleep is the trigger","x":"Protect sleep: early pain relief, a side room, partner staying, and one night feed done by someone else so she gets a 5 to 6 hour block."},{"h":"Quetiapine and glucose","x":"Antipsychotics raise the risk of diabetes in pregnancy. Check weight and BP each visit and offer a glucose tolerance test at 24 to 28 weeks."},{"h":"Never valproate in pregnancy","x":"Valproate is contraindicated in pregnancy for bipolar disorder because of birth defects and developmental harm; MHRA rules restrict starting it in anyone under 55."}],"sim":{"setup":"Thursday morning, joint antenatal clinic. Miss Leah Carter, 30, is 12 weeks pregnant with her first baby. She has bipolar I disorder and is well on quetiapine 300 mg. Her partner Tom is with her. Start her perinatal plan.","role":{"who":"Miss Leah Carter, 30, female, patient (her partner Tom, 33, speaks once if invited)","open":"The midwife said I needed to see you. Is it true I could get ill after the birth?","manner":"Bright but guarded; worried she will be judged or the baby taken. Upset recalling her 2021 admission. Warms when she sees the plan keeps her in control.","free":["Well for three years; working as a teaching assistant","Found out she was pregnant at 6 weeks; planned pregnancy","Wonders if she should stop quetiapine for the baby","Partner Tom is supportive"],"ifasked":["Still takes quetiapine 300 mg every night; missed two doses in early pregnancy with sickness","2021: first signs were sleeping 3 hours, buying expensive things, irritability; later believed she had a special mission","Six weeks in hospital in 2021, on a section for the first two weeks","Maternal aunt has bipolar disorder","Mood and sleep fine now; no suicidal thoughts","Hopes to breastfeed","BMI 27 at booking","Tom can take two weeks' paternity leave; her mum lives 20 minutes away"],"worries":["Will they take the baby away if she gets ill?","Should she stop quetiapine for the baby's sake?","Can she breastfeed on quetiapine?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about 2021 and being sectioned; settles if validated","Bristles at being told what to do; engages when offered choices","Asks about the baby being taken; reassured only by an honest answer about MBUs","Tom speaks once if asked what he would notice first"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, and asks what she knows and wants from today","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear about becoming ill and about losing the baby","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks current mood, sleep, psychotic symptoms and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms quetiapine adherence and her early warning signs from 2021","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about family history of bipolar disorder or postpartum psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains postpartum psychosis risk as about 1 in 5, highest in first 2 weeks","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains quetiapine in pregnancy: no clear birth defect rise, glucose screening, newborn observation","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises continuing quetiapine, explaining why stopping raises relapse risk","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes the written perinatal plan and who receives it by 32 weeks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a birth and postnatal plan that protects sleep, including night feeds","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives her and her partner warning signs and same-day crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises referral, glucose test and next review","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Miss Carter, I'm Dr Osei from the perinatal mental health team. We work with women who've had mental health problems, to plan for pregnancy and after. Congratulations. What would you like from today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"The midwife said I needed to see you. Is it true I could get ill after the birth?"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I'll answer that honestly in a moment. Can I first ask what you've heard?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"That women with bipolar go mad after having babies. And then social services get involved."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a frightening thing to carry into your first pregnancy. I'm sorry. Let's sort fact from fear together.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","band":"How she is","w":"D","s":"How have you been since you found out you were pregnant? Mood, sleep, energy?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Honestly fine. A bit sick, tired, but happy."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Any times feeling very high or racing, or low and hopeless? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"And anything unusual, like hearing things or beliefs others find strange?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"No. Not since 2021."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"Are you still taking the quetiapine every night?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Mostly. I missed two nights when I was being sick. And I've wondered whether I should stop it for the baby."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. We'll talk about that. Can you tell me what happened in 2021?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"It started with not sleeping. Three hours a night, but I felt amazing. Spending loads. Then I thought I had a mission. I was in hospital six weeks. On a section at first."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, especially being sectioned. Thank you for telling me. Those first signs, the sleep and the spending, are really useful for planning.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Tom noticed before I did."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in your family had bipolar disorder, or become unwell after having a baby?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"My aunt has bipolar."},{"t":"2:34","band":"The risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So, your question. Yes, the weeks after birth are a higher-risk time. About 1 in 5 women with bipolar disorder become seriously unwell, usually in the first two weeks. Your aunt's illness nudges that up a little.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"One in five. So I could end up back in hospital."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"It's possible. That also means most women stay well. And because we know now, at 12 weeks, we can plan to lower your risk.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","band":"The medicine","w":"D","s":"About stopping quetiapine: I understand wanting the baby on nothing. Quetiapine hasn't been linked with a clear rise in birth defects. Stopping it makes relapse much more likely, in pregnancy or after the birth.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"So it's safer to keep taking it?"},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"For you, yes, I'd advise continuing 300 milligrams at night. Two things to watch: it can raise blood sugar, so the midwife will book a glucose test at 24 to 28 weeks. And the baby may be a bit unsettled or jittery after birth, so the paediatric team will keep an eye.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"Can I breastfeed on it?"},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Generally yes. Small amounts pass into milk; we'd watch that the baby isn't too sleepy and is feeding well.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"OK. That's better than I thought."},{"t":"4:11","band":"The plan","w":"D","s":"The main tool is a written perinatal plan. It sets out your early signs, your medicines, your birth plan and who sees you after the birth. It's finished by 32 weeks and goes to you, the midwife, the obstetrician, labour ward, your GP and health visitor.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"So everyone knows before I even get there."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Exactly. For the birth, sleep is your biggest trigger. So early pain relief, a side room afterwards, Tom staying, and someone else doing one night feed so you get a five or six hour block.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"I don't want special treatment."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"That's fair, and the plan is yours to shape. It's just that sleep did matter last time.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"I suppose it did."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"After the birth, our team sees you before you go home and regularly for the first two weeks.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:19","w":"P","s":"And if I do get ill? Will they take her?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Having bipolar disorder doesn't mean that. If you needed hospital, there are Mother and Baby Units where you stay together. I'll name the nearest one in your plan.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Tom, what would you notice first if Leah was becoming unwell?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"(Partner) Her sleeping less and talking really fast."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If you see that, ring our team the same day. Out of hours, the crisis team or NHS 111, mental health option. If she's unsafe, 999.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:04","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the main points, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"One in five risk, mostly the first two weeks. Keep taking quetiapine. Sugar test at 24 to 28 weeks. A written plan by 32 weeks, and protect my sleep."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'm referring you to our team today, the midwife will book the glucose test, and I'll see you at 20 weeks to start writing the plan together. How does that sound?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Better. Much better than I expected."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. Ring us any time before then if anything changes.","m":[11,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Validates her fears, confirms adherence and early signs, gives the 1 in 5 risk honestly, keeps quetiapine and sets out a shared written plan that protects sleep.","borderline":"Correct advice but vague on who holds the plan, or no warning signs given to the partner.","fail":"Advises stopping quetiapine, leaves planning until after birth, or gives false reassurance about the postpartum risk."}}},
{"id":"c-p-03","n":1003,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Severe fear of childbirth at 24 weeks after a traumatic first birth","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:00","where":"Perinatal mental health clinic, maternity unit","text":"A 34-year-old woman, 24 weeks into her second pregnancy, has missed two antenatal appointments. Her first birth, three years ago, ended in forceps, a third-degree tear and heavy bleeding. The midwife reports panic attacks at the hospital entrance. She says, 'I can't do that again. Part of me wishes I wasn't pregnant.'"},"task":"Assess her fear of childbirth and any trauma symptoms, check risk, then agree a plan with obstetrics that gives her real choice about how she gives birth.","first60":["Read the first birth notes and the obstetric plan so far; note who her consultant obstetrician is.","Ask the midwife what has been missed: scans, bloods, BP checks.","See her somewhere that is not a labour ward corridor; offer her partner in the room.","Expect a request for a caesarean; know the NICE pathway before you go in.","Plan to ask gently what 'wishes I wasn't pregnant' means: ending the pregnancy, self-harm or suicide."],"ask":["'Can you tell me about your first birth, in your words, from when labour started?'","'What was the worst moment? What did you think was going to happen?'","'Do memories of it come back when you don't want them: nightmares, flashbacks, smells?'","'What do you avoid now because of it?'","'How is your mood, sleep and appetite? Do you still enjoy things?'","'When you say you wish you weren't pregnant, what goes through your mind? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?'","'What do you picture when you think about this birth? What would help you feel safer?'","'Has anyone talked you through what happened last time, or how you'd like to give birth this time?'"],"recognise":["Secondary tokophobia: intense fear of birth after a traumatic previous birth, often with avoidance of antenatal care and requests for caesarean.","Screen for PTSD from the first birth: re-experiencing, avoidance and a persistent sense of threat, with impairment.","Screen for depression and suicidal thoughts; 'wishing I wasn't pregnant' needs gentle, direct questions.","Missed antenatal care is a real risk to her and the baby (missed BP checks, growth scans); the fear is driving it.","Feeling unheard or out of control in labour is as traumatic as the physical events; control and consent are central to the new plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Assess fully, refer to the maternal mental health service for fear-of-childbirth therapy, arrange a joint consultation with a consultant obstetrician and a birth reflections appointment, and support her chosen mode of birth, including a planned caesarean if she still wants one.","r":"Right: NICE asks for specialist support first and an honest discussion of options, but if she still requests a caesarean it should be offered.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her a second vaginal birth is usually easier and she will feel better once labour starts.","r":"Dismissive: it ignores her trauma and choice and makes further missed care more likely.","best":false},{"c":"Book a caesarean today and discharge her from mental health follow-up.","r":"Respects choice but skips the trauma and risk assessment, the informed discussion and treatment she needs for this birth and afterwards.","best":false}],"law":["NICE NG192: if a woman requests a caesarean because of anxiety about birth, offer referral to a professional with expertise in perinatal mental health; if she still wants a caesarean after discussion and support, offer a planned caesarean. An obstetrician unwilling to perform it should refer her to one who will.","Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board (2015): consent must cover the material risks of each option and reasonable alternatives that matter to her.","Consent in labour: she can decline or stop any examination or procedure at any time; record her wishes in the birth plan.","Missed appointments alone are not a safeguarding issue; follow the maternity 'was not brought / did not attend' policy and contact her."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"What happened last time sounds terrifying, and it makes complete sense that your body reacts at the door. You are not being dramatic. Let's make a plan where you have real choices and real control."},{"to":"Patient","s":"If, after talking it through, you still want a caesarean, you can have one. NICE guidance supports that, and I'll make sure the obstetrician hears it."},{"to":"Consultant obstetrician","s":"34-year-old, 24 weeks, secondary tokophobia after forceps, third-degree tear and PPH; PTSD symptoms from the first birth. She requests a planned caesarean. Could you see her within two weeks to discuss options? Perinatal therapy is starting."}],"write":"Perinatal clinic. 34F, G2P1, 24+1 weeks. Referred by midwife: 2 missed appointments, panic at hospital entrance.\nFirst birth 2023: 30 h labour, forceps, 3rd-degree tear, PPH 1.8 L; felt unheard, believed she would die.\nNow: nightmares 2-3/week, flashbacks to theatre lights, avoids hospital, startles, poor sleep. Mood low-ish, enjoys time with son. Fleeting wish not to be pregnant; no thoughts of ending pregnancy, self-harm or suicide.\nImpression: secondary tokophobia with PTSD symptoms from first birth; mild depressive symptoms.\nPlan: MMHS referral (fear-of-childbirth and trauma-focused work); consultant obstetric appointment within 2 weeks re planned CS request; birth reflections appointment; continuity midwife; missed scans rebooked. Review 4 weeks.\nDr L. Hassan ST4","make":["Let her tell the first birth story in her own words; do not correct or explain the obstetric decisions yet.","Ask about the worst moment and what she believed would happen: this is the core of the fear.","Lay out both routes fairly: planned caesarean at 39 weeks, or vaginal birth with a plan built around control.","Build control into any plan: named consultant, continuity midwife, early epidural option, consent before each examination, a stop word, a labour ward visit beforehand.","Rebook the missed checks today with a named midwife, at a quieter clinic if possible."],"next":"Her consultant obstetrician sees her at 26 weeks and agrees a planned caesarean at 39 weeks. She starts six sessions with the maternal mental health service psychologist, attends a birth reflections appointment, and keeps every antenatal check after that.","marks":["Let her tell the first birth story without interrupting.","Screened for PTSD symptoms and depression.","Asked directly about thoughts of self-harm, suicide or ending the pregnancy.","Explained her right to choose, including a planned caesarean.","Referred to therapy and a consultant obstetrician.","Built control and consent into the birth plan."],"pitfalls":["Reassuring that 'second births are easier' before she has been heard.","Treating the caesarean request as the problem to talk her out of.","Missing PTSD from the first birth.","Not asking what 'wishing I wasn't pregnant' means."],"refs":["1. NICE NG192 Caesarean birth (2021; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG116 Post-traumatic stress disorder (2018; checked Nov 2026)","4. Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] UKSC 11","5. RCOG. Third- and fourth-degree perineal tears: patient information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: NHS maternal mental health services treat moderate to severe fear of childbirth, birth trauma and perinatal loss. 'Birth reflections' or 'birth afterthoughts' is a midwife-led service that goes through the birth notes with the woman.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"History to a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Assess her fear of childbirth and trauma symptoms, check risk, and agree birth options with obstetrics.","obj":["Took the first birth history, including the worst moment and what she believed would happen","Screened for PTSD symptoms, depression and thoughts of self-harm, suicide or ending the pregnancy","Explained both birth options fairly and her right to a planned caesarean under NICE guidance","Referred her for fear-of-childbirth therapy and a consultant obstetric review within two weeks","Rebooked missed antenatal checks and agreed a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I thought I was going to die on that table and nobody would tell me anything.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and being left without information made it worse. Thank you for telling me. This time, being told what's happening will be written into your plan.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Everyone keeps telling me the second one's easier. Are you going to say that too?\"","r":"\"No. You've been through something frightening and you deserve to be heard first. Then we'll look at your options properly, including a caesarean.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If they say no to a caesarean, I'll just not come in.\"","r":"\"I hear how desperate you feel. The guidance says if you still want a caesarean after support, you should be offered one. I'll make sure that's discussed with a consultant soon.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I wish I wasn't pregnant. What kind of mother says that?\"","r":"\"A frightened one, and that's understandable. Can I ask what goes through your mind with that? Have you had thoughts of ending the pregnancy or harming yourself?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is a caesarean actually safer for me?\"","r":"\"Each route has different risks. A caesarean avoids labour but means a longer recovery and affects future pregnancies. The obstetrician will give you your own figures so you can choose.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I haven't been to my son's swimming lessons. The pool smells like the hospital.\"","r":"\"That's a really important detail. Avoiding reminders like that is part of how trauma works, and it can be treated. It tells me therapy should start soon.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medicine needed for fear alone; if depression or PTSD is moderate to severe, consider sertraline 50 mg in the morning (compatible with pregnancy and breastfeeding)","Planned caesarean, if chosen, at 39 weeks or later with a pre-operative anaesthetic meeting; or vaginal birth with consultant-led care and early epidural available","Rebook missed checks today: BP, urine, growth assessment as the midwife advises"],"psych":["Maternal mental health service psychologist: fear-of-childbirth sessions (about 6), starting within 2 weeks","If PTSD criteria are met: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, which can be given in pregnancy","Birth reflections appointment with a senior midwife to go through her first birth notes"],"social":["Named continuity midwife; quieter clinic times or community appointments to reduce hospital exposure","Partner at the obstetric appointment and on the labour ward visit","Childcare for her 3-year-old for appointments, via family or the health visitor's local support"],"safety":["Midwife phones within 24 hours of any missed appointment; GP copied in","Crisis contacts: perinatal team in hours, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if unsafe","Review at 28 weeks; written birth plan in the notes by 32 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"NICE says offer the caesarean","x":"Anxiety-based request: offer perinatal mental health support first. If she still wants a caesarean, offer a planned one. An unwilling obstetrician should refer her to a colleague who will."},{"h":"Planned caesarean at 39 weeks","x":"NICE advises planned caesarean birth from 39 weeks unless there is a medical reason for earlier, because babies born earlier have more breathing problems."},{"h":"Control heals","x":"Feeling unheard or powerless in labour drives birth trauma. Write in consent before each examination, a stop word, a named team and clear explanations."},{"h":"Maternal mental health services","x":"NHS services in England for moderate to severe birth trauma, fear of childbirth and perinatal loss, combining psychology with maternity care; refer from midwife, GP or perinatal team."},{"h":"Ask what the wish means","x":"'I wish I wasn't pregnant' may mean fear, ambivalence, thoughts of ending the pregnancy, or suicidal thoughts. Ask each directly and record her words."}],"sim":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, perinatal mental health clinic. Mrs Sarah Doyle, 34, is 24 weeks pregnant with her second baby and has missed two antenatal appointments after a traumatic first birth. Assess her fear and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Sarah Doyle, 34, female, patient","open":"I nearly didn't come in. I'm only here because the midwife rang me twice.","manner":"Tense and defensive at first, expecting to be talked out of a caesarean. Cries describing theatre. Softens and engages once she feels believed and hears she has a real choice.","free":["First birth three years ago: 30-hour labour, forceps, third-degree tear, heavy bleeding","Felt nobody told her what was happening","Panics at the hospital entrance","Wants a caesarean and expects to be refused"],"ifasked":["Nightmares two or three times a week; flashbacks to theatre lights","Avoids her son's swimming lessons because the pool smells like the hospital","Jumpy, always on edge, sleeps about 5 hours","Mood a bit low but enjoys time with her son","'Wishing she wasn't pregnant' is fear; no thoughts of ending the pregnancy, self-harm or suicide","Thought she was going to die and leave her son without a mother","Nobody has ever gone through the first birth notes with her","Husband Mark is supportive but 'doesn't get it'"],"worries":["Will they refuse her a caesarean?","Is a caesarean safer for her?","Will she be judged for missing appointments?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful describing theatre; settles if validated, not hurried","If told second births are easier, becomes angry and quiet","Answers risk questions openly if asked gently and directly","Relaxes once told her choice will be respected"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges how hard it was to come, and agrees the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Lets her tell the first birth story with open questions, without correcting","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress without minimising","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the worst moment and what she believed would happen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for PTSD: re-experiencing, avoidance and sense of threat","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood and asks directly about thoughts of ending the pregnancy, self-harm or suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what she wants for this birth and what would help her feel safer","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains both birth options fairly, without pushing either","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains her right to a planned caesarean under NICE guidance","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Refers for fear-of-childbirth therapy, obstetric review and birth reflections","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Builds control and consent into the birth plan and rebooks missed checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises, checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and a review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Doyle, I'm Dr Hassan from the perinatal mental health team. Thank you for coming in. I know that wasn't easy.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I nearly didn't come in. I'm only here because the midwife rang me twice."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you did. I'd like to understand what's happening for you and plan this birth with you, not for you. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"As long as you're not going to tell me the second one's easier."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"I'm not. I'd like to hear what happened first. Can you tell me about your first birth, in your words?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","band":"First birth","w":"P","s":"I was in labour thirty hours. Then suddenly the room filled with people. Nobody told me anything. They used forceps. I tore badly and bled a lot."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. What happened next?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"They rushed me to theatre. Those lights. I thought I was going to die and leave him without a mum. (She cries.)"},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Take your time. That was a frightening experience, and being left without information made it worse. What you're feeling now makes complete sense.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Everyone says I should be grateful we're both fine."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"You can be grateful and still be hurt by it. Both are true. What was the very worst moment for you?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Lying there bleeding, hearing someone say 'we're losing her'."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me that. That belief, that you were going to die, often stays with people.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"2:04","band":"Trauma now","w":"D","s":"Do memories of it come back when you don't want them? Nightmares, flashbacks?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Nightmares two or three times a week. Any bright light, I'm back in theatre."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you avoid now because of it?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"The hospital. And my son's swimming lessons. The pool smells like the hospital."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"That's really important. Do you feel jumpy or on edge, like something bad is about to happen?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"All the time. I jump at everything. I sleep about five hours."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Do you still enjoy things?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"A bit low. But I love time with my son."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"You told the midwife part of you wishes you weren't pregnant. What goes through your mind with that?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"I'm just so scared of the birth. I want this baby. I just don't want to give birth."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts of ending the pregnancy, or of harming yourself or ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd never leave my son."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you for answering so openly. It sounds like the wish is about fear, not about the baby.","m":[3,6]},{"t":"3:46","band":"Her wishes","w":"D","s":"When you picture this birth, what do you want, and what would help you feel safer?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"A caesarean. Planned. Knowing when, knowing who. But they'll say no."},{"t":"4:01","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Let me be clear about your options. One is a planned caesarean, usually at 39 weeks. It avoids labour, but recovery is longer and it affects future pregnancies. The other is a vaginal birth with a plan built around you: a named consultant, early epidural, and nothing done without your consent.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"And if I still want the caesarean?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Then you can have one. NICE guidance says that if you still want a caesarean after support and a proper discussion, you should be offered one. The obstetrician gives you your own figures, and the choice is yours.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"Really? Nobody's said that."},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"Really. Getting help with the fear matters either way, for this birth and for afterwards.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:00","band":"The plan","w":"D","s":"So, three things. I'll refer you to the maternal mental health service: a psychologist who specialises in fear of birth and trauma. I'll ask your consultant obstetrician to see you within two weeks. And a senior midwife can go through your first birth notes with you, so you know what happened and why.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I'd like that. I still don't know why it all happened."},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"Whatever you choose, we'll write control into the plan: one named midwife, being told what's happening, consent before each examination, and a word you can use to stop and talk.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"That's what I needed last time."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"And the checks you missed matter for you and the baby. Could the midwife see you at the community clinic this week instead of the hospital?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. That I can do."},{"t":"6:08","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're taking away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I'm not mad, I've got trauma symptoms. I can ask for a caesarean. Therapy, the consultant and the notes."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If things get worse before then, call our team; out of hours, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option. I'll see you again in four weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel listened to for the first time."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. You've done the hardest part by coming in.","m":[3,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Hears the first birth fully, screens PTSD, mood and risk, explains both options and her right to a caesarean, and refers to therapy and obstetrics with control built in.","borderline":"Kind and accurate but misses the risk question, or plans without asking what she wants.","fail":"Tries to talk her out of a caesarean, minimises the first birth, or misses PTSD and risk."}}},
{"id":"c-p-04","n":1004,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🍼","title":"Breastfeeding on lamotrigine and sertraline: the choices and watching the baby","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:00","where":"Postnatal ward, maternity unit (perinatal team visit)","text":"A 28-year-old woman with bipolar II disorder gave birth to a healthy term baby girl yesterday. She takes lamotrigine 200 mg and sertraline 100 mg daily and has been well for two years. A healthcare assistant told her she 'can't breastfeed on those tablets'. She is in tears and has not fed the baby since."},"task":"Explain the feeding choices on lamotrigine and sertraline, help her decide, and set out how the baby will be monitored, without any gap in her own treatment.","first60":["Ask the midwife: is the baby term, well, feeding and passing urine? Any jaundice or concerns from the paediatricians?","Check the drug chart: lamotrigine and sertraline given today? No missed doses?","Check the dose was not changed in pregnancy; if it was raised, it needs a planned reduction now.","Look up both drugs on the SPS UK Drugs in Lactation Advisory Service pages before you go in.","Ask the infant feeding team to be available after you have spoken."],"ask":["'What were you told, and how are you feeling about it?'","'How did you hope to feed her before this?'","'Have you had both tablets today? Any missed doses since the birth?'","'How have you slept since she arrived?'","'Any changes in mood, energy or thoughts since the birth?'","'Was your lamotrigine dose changed at all during pregnancy?'","'Who's at home with you, and could anyone do a night feed?'"],"recognise":["Both drugs are generally considered compatible with breastfeeding in a healthy term baby, with monitoring.","Sertraline: very low levels in milk and in babies; a preferred antidepressant for breastfeeding.","Lamotrigine passes into milk in larger amounts and babies clear it slowly; watch for drowsiness, poor feeding, poor weight gain, breathing pauses and rash.","Preterm, unwell or jaundiced babies are more vulnerable; involve the paediatrician.","Stopping or pausing either drug to breastfeed risks relapse; stopping lamotrigine for about a week means re-titrating from the start dose because of rash risk.","Sleep loss after birth can trigger relapse in bipolar disorder; the feeding plan should protect sleep."],"decide":[{"c":"Continue both medicines unchanged, support breastfeeding if she wants it, ask a partner to give one expressed-milk feed at night, and set out baby monitoring with the midwife, health visitor and GP.","r":"Right: it corrects the misinformation, keeps her well, respects her choice and makes monitoring someone's job.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her to stop lamotrigine while breastfeeding and restart when she stops feeding.","r":"Unsafe: high relapse risk after birth, and restarting at 200 mg after a gap risks serious rash; it must be re-titrated.","best":false},{"c":"Advise formula feeding to be 'on the safe side'.","r":"Wrong as a default: both drugs are generally compatible. Formula is a valid choice if she prefers it, but not because of misinformation.","best":false}],"law":["Shared decision-making (GMC 2020; NICE CG192): discuss the benefits of breastfeeding and the possible risks to the baby, and support the choice she makes.","She has capacity unless shown otherwise (MCA 2005); feeding is her decision.","Correct the misinformation and record it; raise it with the ward manager so staff training can follow, without blaming the individual.","With her consent, the plan goes to the midwife, health visitor and GP so the baby's checks are coordinated."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm so sorry you were told that; it wasn't right. Both your tablets are generally considered compatible with breastfeeding for a healthy baby like yours. You can breastfeed if you want to, and we'll keep a close eye on her."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Whatever you choose, breast, bottle or both, keep taking both tablets every day. Stopping them is the bigger risk right now."},{"to":"Midwife","s":"She's breastfeeding on lamotrigine 200 mg and sertraline 100 mg. Please watch the baby for sleepiness, poor feeding, breathing pauses or rash, weigh her as per routine, and call the paediatrician if any appear."}],"write":"Perinatal team review, postnatal ward. 28F, day 1 after SVD, term baby girl, well. Bipolar II; well 2 years on lamotrigine 200 mg and sertraline 100 mg; dose unchanged in pregnancy.\nTold by staff she could not breastfeed; distressed, has not fed since.\nMSE: tearful, appropriate; no elation, no psychotic symptoms, no suicidal ideation. Slept 3 h.\nDiscussed: both drugs generally compatible with breastfeeding (SPS UKDILAS); sertraline very low infant exposure; lamotrigine higher, monitor baby. Options: breast, mixed, formula. Teach-back correct.\nPlan: continues both drugs unchanged. Chooses breastfeeding; partner to give one expressed feed at night. Infant feeding team today. Midwife and HV to monitor baby (sleepiness, feeding, weight, apnoea, rash). Paediatric review if concerns. Ward manager informed of advice given. Perinatal team review day 7.\nDr R. Begum CT2","make":["Apologise for the wrong advice and give the correct position in one sentence before anything else.","Offer the three real choices: breastfeeding, mixed feeding, formula; all safe for her medicines.","Name what to watch in the baby: very sleepy, not waking for feeds, poor weight gain, breathing pauses or colour change, rash.","Protect sleep: one night feed by her partner with expressed milk gives her a 5 to 6 hour block.","Give her the Breastfeeding Network drug factsheets and the SPS lactation pages as written backup.","Ask the infant feeding team to help with the first feed today."],"next":"She breastfeeds with help from the infant feeding lead that afternoon. Her partner gives a bottle of expressed milk at 2 am. At day 5 the baby has regained weight steadily, is alert and feeding well, and the health visitor takes over monitoring.","marks":["Corrected the wrong advice with an apology.","Stated both drugs are generally compatible with breastfeeding.","Gave all three feeding choices without pressure.","Named the baby's warning signs and who checks them.","Kept both medicines unchanged and warned against stopping lamotrigine.","Built sleep protection into the feeding plan."],"pitfalls":["Telling her to stop lamotrigine to breastfeed.","Recommending formula 'to be safe' without facts.","Forgetting to monitor the baby or saying who does it.","Ignoring her sleep in a woman with bipolar disorder."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. Specialist Pharmacy Service. UK Drugs in Lactation Advisory Service: lamotrigine; sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: Lamotrigine; Sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","4. The Breastfeeding Network. Drugs in breastmilk factsheets (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK maternity units have an infant feeding lead and follow UNICEF Baby Friendly standards. For any drug in breastfeeding, the SPS UK Drugs in Lactation Advisory Service is the standard NHS source; quote it in your note.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain feeding choices on lamotrigine and sertraline, agree one with her, and set out the baby's monitoring.","obj":["Corrected the wrong advice and explained that both medicines are generally compatible with breastfeeding","Offered breastfeeding, mixed feeding and formula as real choices and supported her decision","Named the baby's warning signs and who will check feeding, weight and alertness","Kept both medicines unchanged and explained why lamotrigine must not be stopped and restarted","Agreed a night-feed plan that protects her sleep"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why did she tell me I couldn't? I've wasted a whole day.\"","r":"\"You've every right to be upset, and I'm sorry. That advice wasn't correct. I'll raise it with the ward manager. Let's get you feeding her today if that's what you want.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm poisoning her by taking these, aren't I?\"","r":"\"No. You're keeping yourself well so you can care for her. A small amount does reach the milk, which is why we watch her, but for a healthy baby the benefits of breastfeeding usually outweigh that.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Could I just stop the lamotrigine for a few months while I feed?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd think that. The months after birth are when relapse is most likely. And if lamotrigine stops for about a week, it has to be built up again slowly because of rash risk.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What exactly am I watching for?\"","r":"\"If she's unusually sleepy, not waking for feeds, not gaining weight, has pauses in her breathing or a rash. Any of those, tell the midwife or health visitor that day.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I don't breastfeed, will I bond with her?\"","r":"\"Yes. Bonding comes from holding, talking and responding to her, however she's fed. Breast, bottle or both are all fine choices.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I haven't slept since she was born. I'm scared I'll go high.\"","r":"\"That's a really sensible worry, and I'm glad you said it. Let's plan a night feed your partner can do with expressed milk, so you get a proper block of sleep.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue lamotrigine 200 mg daily and sertraline 100 mg in the morning, unchanged; no missed doses","If lamotrigine stops for more than 5 half-lives (about a week), restart at the titration start dose, not 200 mg","If the dose was raised in pregnancy, plan a step-down after birth with the perinatal pharmacist; watch for dizziness, double vision, unsteadiness","Baby: midwife checks feeding and alertness daily; routine weighing around day 5 and day 10 to 14; paediatrician if concerns"],"psych":["Perinatal team review on day 7 and regularly through the first 6 weeks, checking sleep and early signs of hypomania or depression","Teach-back of the baby's warning signs before discharge"],"social":["Infant feeding lead to support the first feed today","Partner to give one expressed-milk feed at night; breast pump provided or loaned before discharge","Health visitor new birth visit (day 10 to 14) to include feeding and weight; GP 6 to 8 week check"],"safety":["Written plan to midwife, health visitor and GP with the baby's warning signs","Her own early signs: sleeping less without tiredness, racing thoughts; perinatal team same day, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours","Ward manager informed of the incorrect advice"]},"facts":[{"h":"Sertraline is a first choice","x":"Very little sertraline reaches breast milk and babies' blood levels are usually undetectable. It is a preferred antidepressant for breastfeeding."},{"h":"Lamotrigine: breastfeed and watch","x":"More lamotrigine reaches the baby than sertraline. A healthy term baby can usually breastfeed; watch for sleepiness, poor feeding, poor weight gain, breathing pauses and rash."},{"h":"Never restart at full dose","x":"If lamotrigine stops for more than 5 half-lives (about a week), restart from the titration start dose. A full-dose restart raises the risk of serious rash."},{"h":"Doses can need cutting after birth","x":"Lamotrigine clearance rises in pregnancy and falls back within weeks of birth. If the dose was raised, plan a reduction with the pharmacist to avoid toxicity."},{"h":"Use the lactation service","x":"For any medicine in breastfeeding, check the SPS UK Drugs in Lactation Advisory Service pages or phone their advisers, and record what you used."}],"sim":{"setup":"Monday late morning, postnatal ward. Mrs Amira Hussain, 28, has bipolar II disorder and had a baby girl yesterday. She takes lamotrigine 200 mg and sertraline 100 mg. Staff told her she can't breastfeed. Explain her choices and the plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Amira Hussain, 28, female, patient","open":"Are you the one who's going to tell me properly? Because someone told me I can't feed my own baby.","manner":"Upset and angry, then guilty and tearful about 'poisoning' the baby. Calms and becomes practical once the facts are clear and her choice is respected.","free":["Baby girl Noor, born yesterday at 39 weeks, healthy","Told by a healthcare assistant she can't breastfeed on her tablets","Has not fed Noor since; midwife gave formula","Really wanted to breastfeed"],"ifasked":["Took both tablets this morning; no missed doses","Lamotrigine dose stayed at 200 mg all pregnancy","Slept about 3 hours since the birth","No elation or racing thoughts so far; mood tearful but OK; no thoughts of self-harm","Last hypomania two years ago started with poor sleep","Husband Karim can do a night feed","Has thought about stopping lamotrigine to feed"],"worries":["Is she harming the baby?","What should she watch for?","Can she stop lamotrigine while breastfeeding?","Will she bond if she can't breastfeed?"],"rules":["Angry for the first minute; calms if the doctor apologises and does not defend the advice","Tearful and guilty about 'poisoning' the baby; settles if validated","Asks to stop lamotrigine; accepts the reason once the rash risk and relapse risk are explained","Mentions poor sleep only if asked"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her distress and apologises for the wrong advice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she was told and what she hoped for feeding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks medicines taken, missed doses and any dose change in pregnancy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks her sleep, mood and early signs of relapse since the birth","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States both medicines are generally compatible with breastfeeding for a healthy term baby","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains sertraline's very low exposure and lamotrigine's higher exposure needing monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers breastfeeding, mixed feeding and formula as real choices without pressure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names the baby's warning signs and who checks feeding and weight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises continuing both drugs and explains lamotrigine re-titration if stopped","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a night-feed plan with her partner to protect her sleep","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and fears about harming or bonding with the baby","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises, arranges feeding support and follow-up","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Hussain, I'm Dr Begum from the perinatal mental health team. Congratulations on Noor. The midwife said you've been given some upsetting advice.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Are you the one who's going to tell me properly? Because someone told me I can't feed my own baby."},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That must have been awful on her first day. What exactly were you told?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"That my tablets go into the milk and I'd hurt her. So she's had formula. I really wanted to breastfeed."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll be clear straight away: that advice wasn't right. Both your tablets are generally considered compatible with breastfeeding for a healthy baby like Noor. I'll raise what happened with the ward manager.","m":[1,5]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"So why did she say it?"},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"It's a common misunderstanding. A lot of people assume any medicine rules it out. I'm sorry it happened to you.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:26","band":"Her medicines","w":"D","s":"Can I check a few things? Did you take both tablets this morning?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Yes, lamotrigine 200 and sertraline 100."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Any missed since the birth? And did the lamotrigine dose change in pregnancy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"No missed doses. It stayed at 200 the whole time."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Good, so no need to adjust it now. How have you been sleeping?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"About three hours since she was born. I'm scared I'll go high."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"That's a really sensible worry. Any racing thoughts, feeling unusually energetic, or very low?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"No, just teary and tired. And no dark thoughts, before you ask."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. We'll come back to sleep, because it matters for you.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:40","band":"The facts","w":"P","s":"But they do go into the milk, don't they? I'm poisoning her."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"I can hear how guilty you feel, and you're not poisoning her. You're keeping yourself well so you can care for her. Small amounts do reach the milk, and that's why we keep an eye on her.","m":[11,6]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"How much?"},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Sertraline hardly at all; in most babies it can't even be measured. Lamotrigine more, and babies clear it slowly. For a healthy, full-term baby, breastfeeding is usually fine, but we watch her closely.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Could I stop the lamotrigine while I'm feeding? Just to be safe?"},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want to. The next few months are when relapse is most likely. And if lamotrigine stops for about a week, it has to be built up again from a low dose because of a risk of serious rash.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"So please keep taking both every day, whatever you choose for feeding.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","band":"Choices","w":"D","s":"You've got three real choices. Breastfeeding fully, mixing breast and bottle, or formula. All are safe with your medicines, and the choice is yours.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"If I didn't breastfeed, would I still bond with her?"},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Yes. Bonding comes from holding her, talking to her, responding to her. However you feed her. It's clear how much you love her already.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"I want to breastfeed. But I need sleep."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"Then let's do both. Could someone at home give one bottle of expressed milk at night, so you get a five or six hour stretch?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"My husband Karim has already offered."},{"t":"5:17","band":"Watching Noor","w":"D","s":"Here's what to watch for in Noor: unusually sleepy, not waking for feeds, not gaining weight, pauses in her breathing or colour change, or a rash. Any of those, tell the midwife or health visitor that day.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"Who checks her weight?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"The midwife checks feeding daily and weighs her around day five. The health visitor takes over at about two weeks. I'll write to them and the GP with this plan.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the main points, so I'm sure I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"I can breastfeed. Keep taking both. Watch if she's sleepy or not feeding, or a rash. Karim does a night feed."},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll ask the infant feeding lead to come and help with a feed now. If your sleep slips or you feel yourself going high, ring our team that day.","m":[12,4]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like a mum again."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You've been her mum all along. I'll see you on day seven.","m":[11,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Apologises, corrects the advice, gives honest drug facts, offers real choices, names the baby's warning signs and who checks them, and protects her sleep.","borderline":"Correct facts but no plan for monitoring the baby, or forgets her sleep and relapse risk.","fail":"Advises stopping lamotrigine or formula 'to be safe', or leaves the misinformation uncorrected."}}},
{"id":"c-p-05","n":1005,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"Thoughts of harming the baby at 5 weeks: OCD-type or psychotic, and how to reassure safely","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 15:30","where":"Community perinatal mental health clinic (urgent appointment)","text":"The health visitor refers a 33-year-old woman, 5 weeks after the birth of her first baby, a boy. She disclosed 'horrible pictures' in her head of stabbing him and has hidden every knife in the house. She cried and asked whether he would be taken away. She has no psychiatric history."},"task":"Assess the thoughts, tell OCD-type intrusive thoughts apart from psychotic ones, judge the risk to the baby and to her, and reassure her safely with a clear treatment plan.","first60":["Read the referral: exact words used, who is caring for the baby, any concerns from the health visitor about care.","Check the birth notes and GP record: delivery, physical health, any past OCD, anxiety or depression.","Plan to ask about harm to the baby in plain words; asking does not make it worse.","Hold the key question in mind: does she want to act on the thoughts, or is she horrified by them?","Know your next step either way: perinatal CBT pathway if OCD-type; same-day senior review if psychotic features or intent."],"ask":["'Can you tell me about the pictures or thoughts? What exactly comes into your mind?'","'How do you feel when they come? Do they feel like your own thoughts?'","'Have you ever wanted to act on them, or come close to doing so?'","'What do you do to stop them or to keep him safe?'","'Do you ever feel he is not really your baby, or that something has to be done to him or for him?'","'Have you been hearing voices or seeing things other people can't?'","'How is your mood and sleep? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?'","'Who else is around to help with him day to day?'"],"recognise":["Intrusive thoughts about harm coming to the baby are common after birth and, in OCD, are unwanted, horrifying and recognised as her own; they do not increase the risk of harm.","OCD pattern: resistance, avoidance (knives, bathing alone), checking, reassurance-seeking, and shame or fear of being judged.","Psychotic pattern: thoughts that feel justified or come from outside, delusional beliefs about the baby, hallucinations, perplexity, little distress about the thoughts, often with severe sleep loss and mood change.","Depression often co-exists; screen mood and suicide in every case.","Over-reaction (an unnecessary child protection referral) causes harm and stops women disclosing; under-reaction misses psychosis."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain that these are OCD-type intrusive thoughts that do not make harm more likely, refer for CBT with exposure and response prevention, offer sertraline, inform the health visitor and GP with consent, and give clear signs that would change the picture.","r":"Right: it is evidence-based, honest and safe, and keeps her talking to services.","best":true},{"c":"Make an immediate child protection referral because she has thoughts of stabbing the baby.","r":"Wrong here: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with no intent or psychotic features are not a risk to the child. A referral would add shame and teach her to hide thoughts.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure her they're normal and discharge her to the GP.","r":"Too little: she has avoidance, checking and distress that need treatment, and her mood needs follow-up.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: be honest that you would share information if you believed the baby was at risk; these thoughts, on their own, do not meet that bar.","Working Together to Safeguard Children 2023: refer to children's social care when there is reasonable cause to suspect significant harm; record your reasoning when you decide not to refer.","With her consent, share the formulation and plan with the health visitor and GP so responses stay consistent.","NICE CG31 and CG192: offer CBT including exposure and response prevention; consider an SSRI, weighing breastfeeding."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for telling me. Many new mums have thoughts like this, and the fact that they horrify you tells me they're not what you want. This kind of thought doesn't make you more likely to harm him."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I'll always be honest with you: if I thought he was at risk, I'd have to share that. From what you've told me, I don't think he is."},{"to":"Health visitor","s":"Assessed today: OCD-type intrusive harm thoughts, ego-dystonic, no intent, no psychotic features, no safeguarding concern. Please avoid repeated reassurance; I'll refer for CBT with ERP and review in 2 weeks."}],"write":"Urgent perinatal review. 33F, 5 weeks postpartum, first baby, SVD, breastfeeding. No psychiatric history.\nIntrusive images of stabbing baby since week 2, many times daily; horrified, recognises as own thoughts, resists. Hides knives, avoids bathing him alone, checks breathing at night, asks partner for reassurance. Never acted, no intent or urge; actively protective.\nNo delusions about baby, no hallucinations, no perplexity. Sleeps when baby sleeps. Mood low-ish, anhedonia partial. No suicidal ideation. Baby well cared for per HV.\nImpression: perinatal OCD (harm obsessions) with mild depressive symptoms. Risk to baby: low. Risk to self: low. No safeguarding referral; reasoning recorded.\nPlan: psychoeducation given; CBT with ERP via perinatal psychology; sertraline 50 mg mane offered, she will consider. HV and GP informed with consent. Review 2 weeks. Same-day contact if thoughts change in character.\nDr K. Mensah ST5","make":["Ask about the thoughts in her own words, then the key test: how does she feel about them, and has she ever wanted to act?","Ask the psychosis questions plainly: beliefs about the baby, voices, feeling things are strange or unreal.","Name it: intrusive thoughts, common after birth, part of OCD, and not linked to harming the baby.","Explain the trap: hiding knives and seeking reassurance bring short relief but keep the thoughts going.","Give the signs that would change things: wanting to act, feeling the thoughts are right, voices, not sleeping at all."],"next":"She starts weekly CBT with exposure and response prevention with the perinatal psychologist and decides to take sertraline 50 mg. By 12 weeks she bathes her son alone and the thoughts come rarely and pass quickly.","marks":["Asked about the harm thoughts directly and without judgement.","Checked whether the thoughts are unwanted and resisted.","Screened for psychotic features and mood, including suicide.","Explained OCD-type thoughts do not raise the risk of harm.","Made a reasoned decision on safeguarding and recorded it.","Offered CBT with ERP and an SSRI compatible with breastfeeding."],"pitfalls":["Referring to children's social care on the content of the thoughts alone.","Missing psychotic features because she seems calm.","Endless reassurance instead of explanation and treatment.","Forgetting her own mood and suicide risk."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31 OCD and BDD guideline (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. HM Government. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Perinatal OCD: information for patients (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: health visitors are specialist public health nurses who see every family from late pregnancy to age 5. They are often the first to hear disclosures; a clear letter back from you shapes how they respond.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"Characterise the harm thoughts, exclude psychosis and intent, judge risk, and explain the diagnosis and treatment.","obj":["Established that the thoughts are unwanted, distressing, resisted and recognised as her own","Asked directly about intent, urges, psychotic symptoms, mood and suicidal thoughts","Explained that OCD-type intrusive thoughts do not increase the risk of harm to the baby","Made and recorded a reasoned safeguarding decision, with honest limits of confidentiality","Offered CBT with exposure and response prevention, sertraline, and signs that need same-day contact"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to take him off me?\"","r":"\"I can see how frightened you are. Having these thoughts is not a reason to take a baby away. I'll be honest throughout: from what you've told me, he's safe with you.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"What kind of mother thinks about stabbing her own baby?\"","r":"\"A frightened, loving one. These thoughts stick precisely because they're the opposite of what you want. Many new mums have them, and they don't make harm more likely.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've hidden all the knives. My partner thinks I'm mad.\"","r":"\"That makes sense as a way of feeling safer. The trouble is, hiding them tells your brain the thought is dangerous, so it comes back stronger. Treatment helps you undo that, step by step.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do you know I won't do it?\"","r":"\"Because of how you are with these thoughts: you hate them, fight them and protect him. That's the pattern of OCD, not of someone who'll act. I'd be concerned if you wanted to act or felt it was right.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can you just tell me again that I'm not dangerous?\"","r":"\"I understand that need. Reassurance helps for a minute, then the doubt returns. I've told you clearly once; therapy will help you live with the doubt without needing it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I take tablets if I'm breastfeeding?\"","r":"\"Yes. For breastfeeding mums, sertraline is usually our antidepressant of choice; very little reaches the baby. It's an option alongside therapy, and the choice is yours.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer sertraline 50 mg in the morning; review at 2 to 4 weeks; increase by 50 mg steps up to 200 mg if needed (OCD often needs higher doses)","Warn: early nausea, loose stools, jitteriness; anxiety can rise in the first fortnight","Compatible with breastfeeding; baby's feeding and settling checked by the health visitor"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention, weekly, perinatal psychology or NHS Talking Therapies perinatal pathway","Psychoeducation today with an RCPsych perinatal OCD leaflet; partner invited to the next session","Partner coached to stop giving repeated reassurance, kindly"],"social":["Health visitor to continue visits and avoid reassurance loops; GP informed with consent","Peer support: local perinatal peer group or Maternal OCD resources"],"safety":["No safeguarding referral; reasoning recorded in the notes","Same-day contact if she feels urges to act, believes the thoughts are right, hears voices or stops sleeping","Crisis: perinatal team in hours, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours; review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Horror means OCD","x":"OCD harm thoughts are unwanted, resisted and recognised as her own; she avoids and checks to protect the baby. These thoughts are not linked to acting on them."},{"h":"Red flags for psychosis","x":"Thoughts feel justified or come from outside, beliefs about the baby being evil or swapped, voices, perplexity, little distress, severe sleep loss. Same-day senior review."},{"h":"Ask plainly, record verbatim","x":"Ask about intent, urges and past near-misses in plain words. Record her exact phrases; they show your reasoning if anyone later asks."},{"h":"Reassurance feeds OCD","x":"Repeated reassurance and avoidance give short relief and keep the cycle going. Say it clearly once, then treat with exposure and response prevention."},{"h":"Wrong referral does harm","x":"An unneeded child protection referral for OCD thoughts adds shame and teaches women to hide symptoms. Record why you did or did not refer."}],"sim":{"setup":"Friday afternoon, community perinatal clinic. Mrs Grace Whitfield, 33, is 5 weeks after her first baby's birth. She told the health visitor she has pictures of stabbing him and has hidden the knives. Assess the thoughts and the risk, and explain.","role":{"who":"Mrs Grace Whitfield, 33, female, patient","open":"I nearly didn't come. I thought if I said it out loud you'd ring social services.","manner":"Ashamed and frightened; avoids eye contact; sobs at first. Relieved and calmer once told the thoughts are common and not dangerous; then asks for repeated reassurance.","free":["Baby son Leo, 5 weeks old, first baby, breastfeeding","Sudden pictures of stabbing Leo with a kitchen knife","Has hidden every knife in the house","Terrified Leo will be taken away"],"ifasked":["Thoughts started at about 2 weeks; many times a day","Horrified by them; knows they are her own thoughts; has never wanted to act","Won't bath Leo alone; checks his breathing several times a night","Asks partner Ben many times a day if she is a bad mother","No voices, no strange beliefs about Leo; he is definitely her baby","Mood a bit low, still enjoys walks with Leo; sleeps when he sleeps","No thoughts of harming herself or ending her life","Had a spell of checking and washing at university that passed on its own"],"worries":["Will Leo be taken away?","How do you know I won't do it?","Can I take tablets while breastfeeding?"],"rules":["Sobs when first describing the thoughts; settles if validated without hurry","If the doctor reacts with alarm, shuts down and minimises","Asks for reassurance a second time; settles if the doctor explains the reassurance trap kindly","Answers psychosis and risk questions openly if asked plainly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, sets a non-judgemental tone and explains confidentiality honestly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her shame and fear before probing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the thoughts in detail: content, onset, frequency","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes the thoughts are unwanted, resisted and recognised as her own","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about avoidance, checking and reassurance-seeking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about intent, urges and ever acting on the thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for psychotic features: beliefs about the baby, voices, perplexity","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains OCD-type thoughts and that they do not raise the risk of harm","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States the safeguarding decision and its reason clearly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers CBT with ERP and sertraline, noting breastfeeding compatibility","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives signs needing same-day contact, summarises, checks understanding and arranges review","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Whitfield, I'm Dr Mensah from the perinatal mental health team. Thank you for coming. I know it took courage.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I nearly didn't come. I thought if I said it out loud you'd ring social services."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That fear makes complete sense. I'll be honest with you: I would only share things if I believed Leo was at risk. Lots of mums tell us about thoughts like this, and most of the time that isn't the case.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. (She starts to cry.)"},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"Take your time. There's no rush, and nothing you say will shock me.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","band":"The thoughts","w":"P","s":"I get pictures. Of stabbing him. With a kitchen knife. Out of nowhere."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That must be horrible to live with. When did they start, and how often do they come?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"About two weeks after he was born. Now it's many times a day."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"How do you feel when one comes?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Sick. Horrified. I love him so much."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Do they feel like your own thoughts, or as if they're put there from outside?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"They're mine. That's what makes it so awful."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Do you try to push them away?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"All the time. It just makes them worse."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"What do you do to keep him safe from them?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"I've hidden every knife. I won't bath him unless Ben's there. I check he's breathing three or four times a night. And I keep asking Ben if I'm a bad mum."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"That all makes sense as ways to feel safer. I'll come back to them. Now I need to ask something plainly. Have you ever wanted to act on these thoughts, or come close to doing so?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd die first."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Any urge at all, even for a moment?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"No. It's the opposite. I'm scared of the thought."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Other experiences","w":"D","s":"Some mums after birth feel their baby isn't really theirs, or that something needs to be done to him. Has anything like that happened?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"No. He's definitely mine. He's perfect."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Have you been hearing voices or seeing things others don't?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Sleep? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"A bit low. I sleep when he sleeps. I still like our walks. No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before, in any form?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"At uni I went through a phase of checking and washing. It passed."},{"t":"3:49","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What you're describing is called intrusive thoughts, part of a condition called OCD. It's common after birth. The thoughts stick because they're the opposite of what you want. Thoughts like this don't make it more likely you'll harm him.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"How do you know I won't do it?"},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Because of how you respond to them. You hate them, fight them and protect him. That's the pattern of OCD. I'd worry if you wanted to act, felt the thoughts were right, or heard voices. None of that is happening.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"So you're not going to call social services?"},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"No. From what you've told me, Leo is safe with you, and I'll write that reasoning in your notes.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Can you just say again that I'm not dangerous?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"I understand the need. Reassurance helps for a minute, then the doubt comes back. That's how OCD keeps going. Hiding knives works the same way. I've said it clearly once; treatment helps you cope without needing it.","m":[9,2]},{"t":"5:14","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"The main treatment is a type of talking therapy called CBT with exposure and response prevention. Step by step, you'd reduce the hiding and checking. I'll refer you to our psychologist this week.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"And tablets? I'm breastfeeding."},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"Sertraline is the one we usually choose for breastfeeding. Very little reaches the baby. We'd start at 50 milligrams in the morning. It can make you a bit sick or jittery for the first week or two. It's your choice, with or without therapy.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"I'll think about it."},{"t":"5:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Please contact us the same day if the thoughts change: if you feel an urge to act, if they start to feel right, if you hear voices, or you stop sleeping. Out of hours, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're taking away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"They're OCD thoughts. They don't mean I'll do it. Therapy, maybe sertraline. Call if they change."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly. With your agreement I'll update your health visitor and GP, and I'll see you in two weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like I can breathe."}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits ego-dystonic, resisted thoughts with avoidance, excludes intent and psychosis, screens mood and suicide, explains OCD safely and plans CBT with ERP.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but thin psychosis screen, or repeated reassurance instead of explanation.","fail":"Makes a safeguarding referral on content alone, misses psychotic features or suicide risk, or reassures and discharges without treatment."}}},
{"id":"c-p-06","n":1006,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"Birth trauma: PTSD three months after an emergency caesarean","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 09:30","where":"Maternal mental health service clinic (birth trauma pathway)","text":"The GP refers a 27-year-old woman 3 months after an emergency caesarean under general anaesthetic; her daughter spent 4 days in neonatal intensive care. She has nightmares, could not walk into the GP surgery for her baby's 8-week vaccinations, and says, 'I don't feel like her mum.'"},"task":"Take a history to a diagnosis of PTSD, check mood, risk and bonding, and explain trauma-focused therapy and what happens next.","first60":["Read the referral and the birth summary: why the emergency, the anaesthetic, the baby's NICU stay, any complications for her.","Check the health visitor record: baby's growth, vaccinations missed, any concerns about care.","Ask whether she would like her partner in for part of the appointment.","Let her know she can pause at any point; talking about the birth can bring on symptoms.","Have the treatment options ready: trauma-focused CBT, EMDR, birth reflections, parent-infant support."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what happened, only as much as feels OK today?'","'What was the worst moment for you?'","'Does it come back when you don't want it to: nightmares, flashbacks, feeling it's happening again?'","'What do you avoid because it reminds you?'","'Do you feel on guard, jumpy, or that something bad is about to happen?'","'How is your mood? Do you blame yourself for anything?'","'Have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life? Any thoughts of harming her?'","'You said you don't feel like her mum. Can you tell me more about that?'"],"recognise":["PTSD (ICD-11): re-experiencing in the present (flashbacks, nightmares), avoidance of reminders, and a persistent sense of current threat (hypervigilance, startle), for several weeks, with real impairment.","Birth trauma is common after emergency birth, general anaesthetic and separation from the baby; feeling powerless or unheard adds to it.","Depression often co-exists; screen mood, guilt and suicide.","'I don't feel like her mum' points to bonding difficulty, often from separation and avoidance; it improves with treatment and needs gentle exploration, not alarm.","Avoidance is now affecting the baby's care (missed vaccinations), which needs a practical plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Make the diagnosis, explain it, refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR in the maternal mental health service, arrange a birth reflections appointment and parent-infant support, and solve the vaccination problem with the health visitor.","r":"Right: NICE first-line treatment, plus practical help that tackles avoidance and bonding.","best":true},{"c":"Start sertraline and review in 3 months.","r":"Not first line: NICE advises trauma-focused therapy first; medication is for those who prefer it or have significant depression.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her to wait, as symptoms after birth often settle by themselves.","r":"Too late for that: at 3 months with impairment, she meets criteria and needs treatment now.","best":false}],"law":["NICE NG116: offer trauma-focused CBT, or EMDR for non-combat trauma more than 3 months ago, to adults with PTSD; offer drug treatment (an SSRI such as sertraline, or venlafaxine) only if she prefers it.","Confidentiality: with her consent, share the plan with the GP and health visitor; a missed vaccination is not by itself a safeguarding matter.","She can ask for her maternity records (UK GDPR subject access) and request a birth reflections review.","If she wants to raise concerns about her care, she can contact the trust's Patient Advice and Liaison Service (PALS); this does not affect her treatment."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"What you're describing has a name: post-traumatic stress disorder, after a frightening birth. It's not weakness, and it's not your fault. It's very treatable."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Not feeling like her mum yet is common after you've been separated and frightened. It usually gets better as the trauma is treated, and we can help with that directly too."},{"to":"Health visitor","s":"She has PTSD after an emergency caesarean and is avoiding the surgery. Could you offer the baby's vaccinations at home or a quiet first slot, and keep visiting while therapy starts? No concerns about care of the baby."}],"write":"Maternal mental health service. 27F, 3 months after category 1 CS under GA for fetal bradycardia; baby NICU 4 days. Breastfeeding stopped at 6 weeks.\nRe-experiencing: nightmares 4/week, flashbacks to waking alone. Avoidance: GP surgery, hospital route, talking about birth; baby's 8-week vaccinations missed. Threat: hypervigilant checking, startle, sleep 4-5 h.\nMood low, guilt ('my body failed her'), enjoys some things. No suicidal ideation; no thoughts of harming baby. Bonding: 'doesn't feel like her mum'; cares for baby well per HV.\nImpression: PTSD (birth trauma), with depressive symptoms; bonding difficulties secondary.\nPlan: trauma-focused CBT (or EMDR, her preference) with MMHS psychologist; birth reflections appointment; parent-infant work; HV to arrange vaccinations at home. GP and HV informed with consent. PHQ-9 and PCL-5 baseline. Review 4 weeks.\nDr J. Fraser CT3","make":["Let her set the pace; ask for the worst moment rather than every detail.","Cover the three PTSD clusters in order: re-experiencing, avoidance, sense of threat; then impairment and duration.","Ask plainly about suicide and thoughts of harming the baby, then explore 'I don't feel like her mum' gently.","Explain trauma-focused therapy: about 8 to 12 weekly sessions, going back to the memory safely so it becomes a memory, not a re-living.","Use a baseline measure (for example PCL-5 or the City Birth Trauma Scale) and repeat it in therapy."],"next":"She chooses EMDR with the maternal mental health service psychologist and attends a birth reflections appointment where a consultant explains the bradycardia and why a general anaesthetic was needed. The health visitor gives the vaccinations at home. By session 8, the nightmares have stopped and she says she feels 'like her mum now'.","marks":["Let her set the pace and covered the worst moment.","Covered re-experiencing, avoidance and sense of threat.","Screened mood, suicide and thoughts of harming the baby.","Explored bonding without alarm.","Explained PTSD and trauma-focused CBT or EMDR.","Fixed the missed vaccinations with the health visitor."],"pitfalls":["Asking for every detail of the birth at the first meeting.","Offering medication as first-line treatment.","Treating 'I don't feel like her mum' as a safeguarding issue rather than a symptom.","Forgetting the partner, who may also be traumatised."],"refs":["1. NICE NG116 Post-traumatic stress disorder (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: 6B40 Post-traumatic stress disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England. Maternal mental health services (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: NHS maternal mental health services, birth reflections clinics and PALS are routine parts of UK maternity care. Women often feel they 'should be grateful'; naming birth trauma as a recognised condition is often the turning point.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Take a trauma history to a PTSD diagnosis, screen risk and bonding, and explain trauma-focused therapy.","obj":["Elicited re-experiencing, avoidance and sense of threat, with duration and impact, and made the diagnosis of PTSD","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming the baby, and explored bonding","Explained PTSD after birth in plain words, without blame","Explained trauma-focused CBT and EMDR, with number of sessions and who delivers them","Agreed practical steps: birth reflections, parent-infant support and the baby's vaccinations"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My body failed her. A proper mum would have given birth normally.\"","r":"\"I'm really sorry you're carrying that. Nothing you did caused her heart rate to drop. Your body got her here safely with help. That guilt is part of the trauma, and therapy helps with it.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I woke up and she was gone. Nobody told me if she was alive.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. Waking up alone, not knowing, is one of the hardest things a mother can go through. Thank you for telling me. Take all the time you need.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I say I don't feel like her mum, will you report me?\"","r":"\"No. It's common after being separated and frightened, and it's a symptom we can treat. You're caring for her well. Telling me helps me help you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Everyone says I should just be grateful she's alive.\"","r":"\"You can be grateful and still be hurt by what happened. Both are true. Being told to be grateful can make it harder to ask for help.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Won't talking about it make it worse?\"","r":"\"It's a fair worry. In therapy you go back to the memory in a planned, safe way, at your pace. That's what makes it lose its power. Avoiding it keeps it raw.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going back into that hospital for anything.\"","r":"\"That makes sense with how you're feeling. We can see you here, away from the maternity unit. As therapy goes on, we'll plan any visits with you, at your pace.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medicine first line; if she prefers medication or depression is significant: sertraline 50 mg in the morning, review at 2 to 4 weeks, up to 200 mg","Physical check at the GP: wound healing, pain, iron, thyroid if fatigue is marked","Sleep: regular routine; avoid alcohol and over-the-counter sedatives"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, 8 to 12 weekly sessions, maternal mental health service psychologist; start within 4 weeks","Birth reflections appointment with a consultant obstetrician or senior midwife to go through her notes","Parent-infant work (for example video interaction guidance) via the perinatal team if bonding remains hard","Baseline PCL-5 and PHQ-9, repeated at session 4 and at the end"],"social":["Health visitor to give the baby's vaccinations at home or arrange a quiet first slot","Partner invited to a session; signpost him to support, as partners can be traumatised too","Birth trauma charity and local peer support group details given"],"safety":["No current risk to self or baby; recorded","Contact the team the same day if suicidal thoughts, thoughts of harming the baby, or not coping with her care","Crisis: NHS 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if unsafe; review in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three clusters, then impact","x":"ICD-11 PTSD: re-experiencing now (flashbacks, nightmares), avoidance, and a sense of current threat, lasting several weeks with impairment. Ask each cluster in turn."},{"h":"Therapy first, not tablets","x":"NICE: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR is first line, 8 to 12 sessions. Drugs (an SSRI such as sertraline, or venlafaxine) only if she prefers them."},{"h":"Bonding follows the trauma","x":"Not feeling like the baby's mother is common after separation and fear, and usually improves with treatment. Explore it gently; it is not a safeguarding trigger alone."},{"h":"Birth reflections helps","x":"Going through the notes with a senior clinician fills gaps in memory and answers 'why'. Offer it to every woman with birth trauma who wants it."},{"h":"Fix the avoidance now","x":"Avoidance can hit the baby's care, such as missed vaccinations. Solve it practically: home visits or quiet slots, while therapy starts."}],"sim":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, maternal mental health service clinic. Ms Chloe Bennett, 27, had an emergency caesarean under general anaesthetic 3 months ago; her daughter was in NICU. She has nightmares and avoids the GP surgery. Assess and explain the treatment.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Bennett, 27, female, patient","open":"My GP said I should come. I don't really know what you can do. It's not like you can change what happened.","manner":"Flat and guarded, then tearful describing waking alone. Ashamed of not feeling like a mum. Opens up and becomes hopeful once she hears it has a name and a treatment.","free":["Emergency caesarean under general anaesthetic 3 months ago","Daughter Isla was in NICU for 4 days","Has nightmares about the birth","Missed Isla's 8-week vaccinations because she couldn't go into the surgery"],"ifasked":["Worst moment: waking alone, nobody telling her if Isla was alive","Nightmares four times a week; flashbacks when she hears a siren or smells hand gel","Avoids the hospital road, the GP surgery and talking about the birth","Checks Isla constantly; jumps at noises; sleeps 4 to 5 hours","Mood low; guilt that her 'body failed'; still enjoys some time with her sister","No thoughts of suicide; no thoughts of harming Isla","'Doesn't feel like her mum': feels Isla bonded with her partner Jamie first","Stopped breastfeeding at 6 weeks"],"worries":["Will talking about it make it worse?","Will she be reported for not feeling like Isla's mum?","Will she have to go back into the hospital?"],"rules":["Tearful describing waking alone; settles if validated and given time","If asked for every detail, becomes overwhelmed and goes quiet","Discloses 'not feeling like her mum' only if asked gently and told it is common","Becomes hopeful when told the condition has a name and responds to treatment"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and lets her set the pace","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the birth story and worst moment without demanding every detail","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her distress and guilt without minimising","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about re-experiencing: nightmares, flashbacks, triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about avoidance and its impact, including the missed vaccinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sense of threat: hypervigilance, startle, sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood and asks directly about suicide and thoughts of harming the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores bonding gently and normalises it","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives the diagnosis of PTSD after birth in plain words, without blame","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains trauma-focused CBT or EMDR: what happens, how many sessions, who delivers it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees birth reflections, parent-infant support and the vaccination plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises, checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and a review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Ms Bennett, I'm Dr Fraser from the maternal mental health service. Thank you for coming. Today I want to understand what's been going on since Isla's birth, and talk about what can help.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"My GP said I should come. I don't really know what you can do. It's not like you can change what happened."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"You're right, I can't change it. But I may be able to change how it affects you now. We'll go at your pace, and you can stop at any point.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:47","band":"The birth","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happened, only as much as feels OK today?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Labour was fine, then her heart rate dropped. Suddenly everyone was running. They put me to sleep. I woke up and she was gone."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. What was the worst moment for you?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Waking up alone. Nobody told me if she was alive. For an hour. (She cries.)"},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Waking up alone, not knowing, is one of the hardest things a mother can go through. Take your time.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Everyone says I should be grateful she's fine."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"You can be grateful and still be hurt by what happened. Both are true.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","band":"Symptoms","w":"D","s":"Does it come back when you don't want it to? Dreams, or feeling it's happening again?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Nightmares four times a week. If I smell hand gel or hear a siren, I'm back there."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you avoid now because it reminds you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"The hospital road. Talking about it. And I couldn't go into the GP surgery. Isla missed her jabs."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, and it tells me how strong the fear is. We'll sort the jabs together.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"I feel like a terrible mum."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"You're not. Avoiding reminders is part of trauma, not a sign of how much you care. Do you feel on edge, as if something bad is about to happen?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"All the time. I check her constantly. I jump at every noise. I sleep maybe four or five hours."},{"t":"3:05","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Do you blame yourself for anything?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Low. I think my body failed her."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Nothing you did caused her heart rate to drop. That guilt is part of the trauma. Have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[3,7]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never leave her."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of harming Isla?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. God, no."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Bonding","w":"D","s":"Your GP said you told her you don't feel like her mum. Can you tell me about that? It's something many mums feel after a birth like yours.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"She was with Jamie first. In NICU. I feel like she's his. I'm scared to say that."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for trusting me with it. After being separated and so frightened, that feeling is common. It usually gets better as the trauma is treated, and we can help directly too.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:24","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"What you're describing has a name: post-traumatic stress disorder, after a traumatic birth. The memory hasn't been filed away as past, so your body reacts as if it's still happening. It isn't weakness, and it's very treatable.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"So I'm not going mad."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"No, you're not. The main treatment is a talking therapy focused on the trauma. Either trauma-focused CBT, or EMDR, which uses eye movements while you hold the memory in mind.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"Won't talking about it make it worse?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"It's a fair worry. You go back to it in a planned, safe way, at your pace. That's what makes the memory lose its power. Usually 8 to 12 weekly sessions with one of our psychologists, here, not at the hospital.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Do I need tablets?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"They're not the first choice. If you'd prefer them, or your mood drops further, sertraline is an option, and we can talk about it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:45","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Three other things. A birth reflections appointment, where a senior doctor goes through your notes and explains why things happened. Some parent and baby sessions to help you and Isla. And I'll ask your health visitor to give Isla's jabs at home.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"At home? That would help so much."},{"t":"6:11","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're taking away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"It's PTSD from the birth. It's treatable. Therapy here, a notes appointment, help with Isla, jabs at home."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If things get worse, or you have thoughts of self-harm or Isla, ring us the same day; out of hours, NHS 111, mental health option. I'll see you in four weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like someone finally gets it."}],"global":{"pass":"Takes the trauma history at her pace, covers all three PTSD clusters, screens mood and risk, normalises bonding, and explains trauma-focused therapy with a practical plan.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but misses the risk questions or the bonding concern, or gives a vague treatment description.","fail":"Pushes for every detail, offers tablets first, treats bonding difficulty as a safeguarding issue, or gives no plan."}}},
{"id":"c-p-07","n":1007,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"Postpartum psychosis: talking with her husband after admission to the mother and baby unit","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 16:30","where":"Family room, regional mother and baby unit (MBU)","text":"Mrs Hannah Lewis, 32, was admitted two days ago under Section 2 with postpartum psychosis, 11 days after the birth of her first baby, Alfie. She has no past psychiatric history. She has agreed that her husband, Tom, 34, can be told about her care. He has asked to speak to a doctor. He looks as if he has not slept for days."},"task":"Explain postpartum psychosis to her husband, give an honest outlook and the risk in a future pregnancy, and agree practical ways he can help her, Alfie and himself.","first60":["Check the notes: her written consent to share with Tom, the Section 2 start date, current medicines and today's nursing report.","Ask the nurse how Hannah and Alfie are this afternoon, so your update is current.","Book the family room, switch your bleep to a colleague for 20 minutes and bring a leaflet on postpartum psychosis.","Start with how he is and what he already understands before you explain anything."],"ask":["'Before I say anything, how are you holding up?'","'What have you been told up to now, and what do you make of it?'","'What did you notice in the days before she came in?'","'Has anyone in Hannah's family had a serious mood illness, or been unwell after having a baby?'","'What worries you most right now?'","'Who is helping you at home with Alfie and with you?'","'Are you sleeping and eating? How is your own mood?'"],"recognise":["Postpartum psychosis affects about 1 to 2 in every 1,000 women after birth, usually within the first two weeks; first-time mothers are at higher risk.","It is a severe mood illness, usually mania, depression or a mixed state with psychosis; many cases sit on the bipolar spectrum.","With treatment most women recover fully. The acute phase usually settles over weeks; full recovery often takes 6 to 12 months, and depression or anxiety after the high phase is common.","After one episode, the chance of another after a future pregnancy is about 1 in 2; a plan made before conception lowers it.","Partners often feel guilt, shock and exhaustion and may develop their own depression or trauma symptoms.","As her husband, Tom is her nearest relative under the MHA 1983 and has rights during Section 2."],"decide":[{"c":"Meet Tom today with her consent, explain the illness and outlook plainly, answer the future-pregnancy question honestly, agree how he can help, and offer him a carer's assessment and peer support.","r":"Correct: an informed, supported partner improves her recovery and the baby's care, and he has a right to information as nearest relative.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him to wait for the ward round on Monday so the consultant can explain.","r":"Unhelpful: he is frightened now, and you can explain the basics safely today and book the consultant meeting too.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him the risk of it happening again is low, so he should not worry about more children.","r":"Wrong and unsafe: the recurrence risk is about 1 in 2; false reassurance removes the chance to plan.","best":false}],"law":["Section 2 MHA 1983: up to 28 days for assessment (and treatment). Hannah can apply to the Tribunal within the first 14 days.","Tom, as her husband, is her nearest relative (s26). He can order her discharge with 72 hours' written notice to the hospital managers; the responsible clinician can bar it if she would be likely to act dangerously (s25).","Hannah has consented to information sharing. Without consent, share only what is needed, and always listen to him (GMC confidentiality guidance).","Under the Care Act 2014 Tom can ask the local authority for a carer's assessment.","MHA 2025 reforms (nominated person replacing nearest relative) are passed but not yet in force (Nov 2026)."],"say":[{"to":"Husband","s":"Hannah has an illness called postpartum psychosis. It is a known and serious illness that can start in the days after a birth. It is not caused by anything you or she did, and most women recover fully."},{"to":"Husband (future pregnancy)","s":"If Hannah has another baby, there is about a one in two chance it could happen again. That sounds high, but with a plan made before the pregnancy, we can lower that chance and act very early."},{"to":"Husband (helping)","s":"The most useful things are short, calm visits, helping her care for Alfie here, and telling us about any change you notice. And looking after yourself, because she and Alfie need you well."}],"write":"16:30 Met husband Tom Lewis (NoK and nearest relative), with Hannah's written consent. Nurse K. Adeyemi present.\nExplained: diagnosis of postpartum psychosis, Section 2, treatment with olanzapine, likely course and full recovery for most.\nDiscussed: risk of about 1 in 2 after a future pregnancy; pre-conception review with perinatal team advised.\nNew info: Hannah's mother had a 'breakdown' needing admission after Hannah's birth. Added to history; family history of probable postpartum psychosis.\nTom: exhausted, low but no thoughts of self-harm; sister helping. Not sleeping.\nPlan: carer's assessment referral, APP partner peer support details given, leaflet given, family meeting with consultant Fri 14:00. Tom to phone nurse in charge with any concerns.\nDr S. Rahman CT2","make":["Start with his state and his understanding; then explain in three blocks: what it is, what treatment does, what recovery looks like.","Use one plain phrase for the illness and stick to it; avoid 'bipolar' until the consultant has discussed it with Hannah.","Give numbers as natural frequencies ('about 1 in 2'), then say what lowers the risk.","Agree two or three concrete jobs for him, not a list of ten.","Check he can repeat back the key points and knows who to call on the unit.","Ask about his own mood, sleep and any thoughts of self-harm; partners are a hidden risk group."],"next":"Tom brings in her own pillow and photos and starts doing Alfie's evening bath on the unit. At the family meeting on Friday, the consultant explains treatment and discusses a future-pregnancy plan with both of them. Tom's GP signs him off work for two weeks.","marks":["Checked consent and asked how he was before explaining.","Explained the illness in plain words and removed blame.","Gave an honest outlook, including the slower recovery and low mood afterwards.","Gave the recurrence risk as about 1 in 2, with what lowers it.","Agreed concrete ways he can help and support for himself.","Asked about family history and his own mental health."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing before finding out what he knows and how he is.","Falsely reassuring about future pregnancies.","Ignoring the partner's own exhaustion, low mood or risk.","Saying 'bipolar disorder' as a fixed diagnosis on day two."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Postpartum psychosis: patient and carer information (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters on nearest relatives and information for families (checked Nov 2026)","4. Action on Postpartum Psychosis (app-network.org): information and peer support for partners (checked Nov 2026)","5. MBRRACE-UK. Saving Lives, Improving Mothers' Care (latest report; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK the partner is a key part of the MBU team, not a visitor to be managed. Expect to give him time, take his worries seriously and involve him in planning, with the patient's consent.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Explain a diagnosis + Family meeting","do":"Explain postpartum psychosis, outlook and 1-in-2 recurrence risk to her husband, and agree three ways he helps.","obj":["Explained postpartum psychosis in plain words, including that it is nobody's fault and that most women recover fully","Gave an honest outlook: weeks for the acute illness, months for full recovery, and possible low mood afterwards","Stated the risk of recurrence after a future pregnancy as about 1 in 2 and named two ways to lower it","Agreed three concrete ways he can help on the unit and at home, and how to contact the team","Checked his own mood, sleep and risk, and offered a carer's assessment and partner peer support"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have seen it. She was up all night talking and I just thought she was excited.\"","r":"\"Lots of partners say exactly that. It came on fast, and early signs look like new-baby excitement. You noticed, and you got her help. That's why she's here now, getting better.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is she going to hurt Alfie?\"","r":"\"That's a natural fear. The staff are with them all the time, and Alfie is cared for safely here. As she gets better, those risks fall. Tell us anything she says that worries you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will she ever be the same Hannah again?\"","r":"\"Most women make a full recovery. The worst usually settles in weeks; getting back to her usual self can take some months, and she may feel low on the way. That's part of recovery, not a relapse.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's not dangerous. Why is she locked up on a Section?\"","r":"\"I understand it feels wrong. She's very unwell and can't see it right now, so the Section lets us treat her safely. It's for up to 28 days, and it's reviewed all the time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can we have another baby?\"","r":"\"Yes, many couples do. The honest answer is there's about a one in two chance it could happen again. Planning before pregnancy, with the perinatal team, lowers that and means help starts the day she gives birth.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I haven't slept in a week. I'm meant to be holding it all together.\"","r":"\"You've had a huge shock, and you're running on empty. Holding it together includes looking after yourself. Can we talk about who can help you this week?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine 5 mg at night, increasing to 10 mg after 2 to 3 days if tolerated; check weight, BMI, fasting glucose and lipids at baseline and at 12 weeks","Lorazepam 0.5 to 1 mg orally as needed for severe agitation, maximum 4 mg in 24 hours; review daily","Bloods already done: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFTs, calcium, glucose, CRP; repeat TFTs at 6 weeks to catch postpartum thyroiditis","MBU pharmacist checks every medicine for breastfeeding; Alfie watched for sleepiness and poor feeding if breastfed"],"psych":["Family meeting with the consultant and named nurse, Friday 14:00, for both of them","Psychoeducation for Hannah and Tom about early warning signs once she is calmer","Offer partner sessions with the MBU psychologist if Tom has intrusive memories of the admission"],"social":["Carer's assessment request to the local authority (Care Act 2014), made by the MBU social worker","Tom given Action on Postpartum Psychosis partner peer support details","Tom to take statutory paternity leave and see his GP about further time off or a fit note","Nursery nurse to support Tom and Hannah with Alfie's care during visits"],"safety":["Tom told how to reach the nurse in charge 24 hours a day and to report any change in her talk or behaviour","Tom's GP told, with his consent, that he is under strain; he knows to ring 111 (mental health option) if he feels unsafe","Health visitor and GP informed of admission; future-pregnancy plan to be written into her discharge summary"]},"facts":[{"h":"About 1 to 2 in 1,000 births","x":"Postpartum psychosis is rare but well known. Onset is usually within two weeks of birth; risk is highest with bipolar disorder, a past episode, or a mother or sister who had it."},{"h":"Recurrence: about 1 in 2","x":"After one episode, about half of women become unwell after a later birth. A pre-conception plan, specialist antenatal care and protected sleep after birth lower the risk."},{"h":"Recovery is slow after fast onset","x":"The acute illness usually settles over weeks. Full recovery takes months, and low mood or anxiety afterwards is common; warn the family so they do not read it as failure."},{"h":"Husband is nearest relative","x":"He can order discharge from Section 2 with 72 hours' written notice; the responsible clinician can bar it. Explain his rights early so he trusts the process."},{"h":"Ask about her mother","x":"A mother or sister with postpartum psychosis raises the risk sharply. Partners often know family history the patient cannot give while unwell."}],"sim":{"setup":"Wednesday 16:30, mother and baby unit. Hannah Lewis, 32, was admitted under Section 2 two days ago with postpartum psychosis, 11 days after her first baby. She has consented to sharing. Her husband wants to talk. Explain the illness and plan with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Tom Lewis, 34, Hannah's husband","open":"Thanks for seeing me. Nobody's really told me what's going on. Is she going to be OK?","manner":"Exhausted, polite and frightened; slightly defensive about the Section. Breaks down briefly when he talks about not spotting it; steadies when reassured it is not his fault, and becomes practical.","free":["She stopped sleeping about four days after Alfie was born, then talked non-stop","On the last night she said Alfie was 'not really ours' and was sending her signals","He has hardly slept for a week and is back at work on Monday","He wants to know whether she will ever be 'herself' again"],"ifasked":["Hannah's mother was in hospital for 'a breakdown' a few weeks after Hannah was born; nobody talks about it","His sister is staying with him and helping","He feels low and keeps replaying the night she was admitted; no thoughts of harming himself","He is drinking two or three beers a night to get to sleep","They had hoped to have a second child in a couple of years","He did not know he could visit outside set times or help with Alfie on the unit"],"worries":["Why is she on a Section if she's not dangerous?","Could she hurt Alfie?","Can we ever have another baby?","Is this my fault for not getting help sooner?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about the night of admission; settles if his guilt is named and lifted","If told the future risk is low, he later says 'so we'll be fine next time?' and relaxes completely (a sign of false reassurance)","Only mentions Hannah's mother if asked about family history","Accepts help for himself only if it is framed as helping Hannah and Alfie"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Hannah's consent to share, and asks how he is before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Finds out what he already knows and what he fears most","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names postpartum psychosis and explains it in plain words, without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States clearly that it is not caused by him or Hannah","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why the Section is in place and his rights as nearest relative","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives an honest outlook: full recovery for most, weeks to settle, months to recover, low mood possible","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the future-pregnancy risk as about 1 in 2 and how planning lowers it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about family history of mental illness after childbirth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees concrete ways he can help on the unit and tells him what changes to report","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks about his sleep, mood, alcohol and thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his distress and guilt before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers carer's assessment, partner peer support and a family meeting with the consultant","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and summarises the plan with a named contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"He leaves understanding the illness, the outlook and the 1-in-2 future risk, free of blame, with clear jobs and support for himself.","borderline":"Accurate explanation but rushed; little attention to his own state, or recurrence risk given vaguely.","fail":"False reassurance about future pregnancies, jargon-heavy lecture, or ignoring his distress and own risk."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Lewis. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the doctors on the unit. Hannah has said she's happy for us to talk about her care with you. Before anything else, how are you holding up?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me. Nobody's really told me what's going on. Is she going to be OK?"},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"I'll answer that properly, I promise. Can I first ask what you've been told so far, and what's worrying you most?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Just that she's got psychosis and she's on a Section. And I keep thinking she might hurt Alfie. Or herself."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Those are frightening things to carry, especially on no sleep. Let's go through them one at a time.","m":[11]},{"t":"0:45","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"What did you notice in the days before she came in?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"She stopped sleeping about four days after he was born. Then she talked non-stop. The last night she said Alfie wasn't really ours, that he was sending her signals."},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"I thought she was just excited. I should have seen it. I should have rung someone days earlier."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much that's weighing on you. It came on fast, and the early signs look a lot like new-baby excitement. You did notice, and you got her help. That's why she's here.","m":[11,4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"Yeah. OK. Sorry."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"No need to be sorry. Take your time.","m":[11]},{"t":"1:30","band":"Explain the illness","w":"D","s":"What Hannah has is called postpartum psychosis. It's a serious illness that can start in the first days or weeks after a birth. It affects about one or two women in every thousand.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"Her mood and thinking have been thrown off course, so she believes things that aren't true, like the idea about Alfie. It's an illness of the brain, a bit like a very high fever of the mind.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"But why her? She's never had anything like this."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Often there's no single reason. The big hormone changes and loss of sleep after birth play a part, and some families are more prone. It isn't caused by anything you or Hannah did.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Can I ask, has anyone in Hannah's family had a serious mood illness, or been unwell after having a baby?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Actually, her mum was in hospital for a breakdown after Hannah was born. Nobody ever talks about it."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful, thank you. It makes this illness more likely, and it'll help us plan. I'll make sure the consultant knows.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:39","band":"The Section","w":"P","s":"She's not dangerous, though. Why has she been sectioned?"},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"I understand it feels wrong. She's very unwell and can't see that she is, so the Section lets us treat her safely. Section 2 lasts up to 28 days and is reviewed all the time.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"As her husband, you're her nearest relative. That gives you rights: to be kept informed, and to ask for her discharge in writing. The team would talk with you first if that ever came up.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"OK. I didn't know that."},{"t":"3:14","band":"Outlook","w":"P","s":"So will she be back to normal? Will she be Hannah again?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Most women make a full recovery. With medicine, sleep and care here, the worst usually settles over a few weeks. Getting fully back to herself often takes months.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Many women feel low or anxious after the high phase passes. That's part of recovery, not going backwards, so please don't be alarmed if you see it.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"And Alfie? Is he safe with her?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"He's cared for here with staff around them all the time, so he's safe. As she gets better, those risks fall. If she says anything that worries you, tell the nurse straight away.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:58","band":"Future pregnancy","w":"P","s":"We'd talked about having another one. Is that off the table now?"},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Many couples do have more children. I'll be honest with you: after one episode, there's about a one in two chance it could happen again after a later birth.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"One in two. God. That's a lot."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"It is a lot, and it's hard to hear today. The good news is planning makes a real difference. Seeing the perinatal team before trying, specialist care in pregnancy, protected sleep, and sometimes medicine straight after the birth.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"So it's not a no. It's a plan made together, well before.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:39","band":"How he can help","w":"P","s":"What can I actually do? I feel useless."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"You're far from useless. Three things help most. Short, calm visits, any time, not just set hours. Helping her care for Alfie here, like bath time with the nursery nurse. And telling us about any change you notice.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"If she says something odd, don't argue with it. Just be calm, and gently move on.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I can do that. I didn't know I could do bath time."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Him","w":"D","s":"And you. How are you sleeping, and how's your mood?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Barely sleeping. I keep replaying that night. I've been having a couple of beers to get off."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"That's understandable after a shock like this. The beer may get you to sleep but it wakes you later. Any thoughts of self-harm or of wishing you weren't here?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. I just need to get through it."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Would you see your GP this week about sleep and time off? You're back at work Monday, and that sounds too soon.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"I'll also ask our social worker to arrange a carer's assessment, and give you details for Action on Postpartum Psychosis. They have partners who've been exactly where you are.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"That would help, actually."},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"And our consultant would like to meet you both on Friday at two, to talk about treatment and that future plan.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, what will you tell your sister tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"That it's postpartum psychosis, it's not our fault, most women get completely better but it takes months. And if we have another baby, we plan it with the team first."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Here's a leaflet, and the nurse in charge's number, any time, day or night. Is there anything I haven't answered?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. That's the first time it's made sense."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me about her mum. Go and see them both, and I'll see you on Friday.","m":[13,1]}]}},
{"id":"c-p-08","n":1008,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"Severe postnatal depression at six weeks: explaining and agreeing a mother and baby unit admission","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:00","where":"Home visit with the community perinatal mental health team","text":"Mrs Amira Haddad, 29, had her first baby, Lina, six weeks ago. She has severe depression: she has lost 5 kg, sleeps two hours a night even when Lina sleeps, and says Lina 'would be better off with someone else'. She has been on sertraline 50 mg for three weeks and the crisis team has visited twice daily for a week, but she is getting worse. Her husband, Karim, 33, is at work. Your consultant agrees she needs a mother and baby unit (MBU) bed."},"task":"Check current risk, explain why an MBU admission is recommended and what it involves, answer her fears honestly, and agree the admission with her.","first60":["Phone the MBU before the visit to confirm a bed, the distance and whether they can take her today.","Read the crisis team notes: risk entries, eating, sleep, the baby's care and what was tried.","Check Lina's weight and feeding with the health visitor's last entry.","Plan to see Amira alone first, then with her mother-in-law if she wants.","Have the MBU leaflet and the unit's phone number ready."],"ask":["'How have the last few days been since the crisis team started coming?'","'You said Lina would be better off with someone else. Can you tell me more about that?'","'Have you had thoughts about ending your life, or of not waking up? Any plans?'","'Have you had any thoughts of harming Lina, even ones that frighten you?'","'How are you managing to feed and settle her?'","'What do you know about mother and baby units? What worries you about going in?'","'Who would you like with you when we talk about this?'"],"recognise":["Red flags in the perinatal period: recent significant change in mental state, new thoughts or acts of violent self-harm, and new persistent feelings of being incompetent as a mother or estranged from the baby.","Suicide is a leading cause of maternal death in the year after pregnancy (MBRRACE-UK); women who die often use violent methods.","Severe depression: weight loss, early waking, guilt, hopelessness and ideas of being a bad mother; check for psychotic features (delusions of guilt or that the baby is harmed or damaged).","Failure of intensive home treatment plus rising risk is a clear reason for admission.","An MBU keeps mother and baby together, supports bonding and feeding, and is NICE's recommended setting for a woman needing admission within 12 months of birth.","Rule out physical causes: anaemia, thyroid disease (postpartum thyroiditis), low ferritin or B12, infection."],"decide":[{"c":"Recommend informal admission to the MBU today with Lina, explain what it involves, address fears about social services honestly, increase sertraline, and arrange transport and family support.","r":"Correct: risk is rising despite home treatment; the MBU is the safest place and keeps them together.","best":true},{"c":"Continue crisis team visits and add a sleeping tablet.","r":"Unsafe: home treatment has already failed and she is expressing estrangement from her baby and passive suicidal ideas.","best":false},{"c":"Admit her alone to the nearest general adult ward so she is closer to home.","r":"Not first choice: separation from her baby may worsen guilt and bonding; NICE recommends a specialist MBU unless there are specific reasons not to.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity to decide about admission and is agreeing: informal admission. Record the capacity and consent discussion.","If she refused and the risk remained high, an MHA assessment could be needed; MBUs admit detained women. Be honest if she asks.","Informal admission is not a safeguarding action. Share with the health visitor, GP and midwife as part of care; refer to children's social care only if there is a specific concern for Lina.","NICE CG192: women needing inpatient care within 12 months of birth should normally be admitted to a specialist MBU, unless there are specific reasons not to."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've been carrying something really heavy, and it's getting harder, not easier, even with the team coming in. I think you and Lina need more help than we can give at home. I'd like you both to go to a mother and baby unit."},{"to":"Patient","s":"It's a hospital ward made for mums and babies. Lina stays with you. Nurses and nursery nurses are there day and night, so you can sleep while they help with her."},{"to":"Patient (social services)","s":"Going to the unit is about getting you well. It isn't a referral to social services. We do tell the health visitor and GP, because they look after you both."}],"write":"11:45 Home visit with CPN L. Brooks. Amira Haddad 29F, 6/52 postnatal, first baby.\nS: Worsening low mood despite CRHT twice daily x 7 days. Sleeps 2 h/night, eating little (5 kg weight loss). Feels Lina 'better off with someone else'. Passive wish not to wake; one thought last week of stepping into traffic, no plan, no intent today. No thoughts of harming Lina. Breastfeeding.\nO: Slowed, tearful, guilt about mothering, no psychotic features elicited. Lina well cared for, mother-in-law present.\nA: Severe depressive episode with postnatal onset, high and rising risk; home treatment failed.\nP: Informal MBU admission today, agreed with capacity. Sertraline 50 mg to 100 mg. Bloods on admission. Husband informed by phone with consent. HV, GP and midwife informed. Transport 15:00 with CPN.\nDr S. Rahman CT2 for Dr P. Nair, consultant","make":["Re-check risk first: suicidal ideas, method, intent, thoughts of harm to the baby, and psychotic features.","Name the change: 'it's getting worse despite the team coming in'.","Explain the MBU in concrete pictures: who is there, a typical day, the baby's cot by her bed, visiting.","Ask what she fears; answer each worry directly and honestly.","Agree the practical plan: when, how she gets there, what to pack, who tells Karim.","Check understanding and consent, and record capacity for the decision."],"next":"Amira agrees, as long as Karim can visit every evening. She and Lina go to the MBU by 16:00. Sertraline is increased to 100 mg, and she sleeps five hours on the first night while the nursery nurse settles Lina.","marks":["Rechecked suicide risk and thoughts about the baby before explaining.","Explained clearly why admission is needed now.","Described the MBU concretely, including that Lina stays with her.","Answered fears about social services and being judged honestly.","Agreed practical arrangements and involved her husband.","Recorded capacity and consent for informal admission."],"pitfalls":["Selling the admission with false promises, such as 'you'll be home in a week'.","Avoiding the question 'what if I say no?'.","Not asking about thoughts of harm to the baby.","Forgetting feeding, packing and transport, which are what she will worry about."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. MBRRACE-UK. Saving Lives, Improving Mothers' Care (latest report; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Perinatal mental health services: recommendations for the provision of services (CR232, 2021; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: there are only a small number of MBUs in the UK, so the nearest may be far from home. Check the bed and the distance before you raise it, and think about how the family will visit.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Explain a treatment + Risk assessment","do":"Recheck risk, explain why a mother and baby unit admission is needed, answer her fears, and agree admission today.","obj":["Rechecked suicidal ideas, intent, thoughts of harm to the baby and psychotic features before recommending admission","Explained why admission is needed now, naming the failure of home treatment and the rising risk","Described what happens on the MBU, including that the baby stays with her and feeding is supported","Answered her fear of social services and of being judged as a bad mother honestly","Agreed informal admission today with capacity recorded, transport and the husband informed"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I go in, they'll take her off me. Social services will be involved.\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd worry about that. Going to the unit is about getting you well, with Lina beside you. It isn't a social services referral. We tell the health visitor and GP, as we always would.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"A proper mum wouldn't need to go to hospital.\"","r":"\"Depression after a baby is an illness, not a test of being a good mum. You noticed you weren't right and let us help. That is exactly what a good mum does.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't the crisis team just come three times a day instead?\"","r":"\"I wish more visits would do it. They've come twice a day for a week and you're feeling worse. The unit gives you help through the night, which is when it's hardest.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if I say no?\"","r":"\"It's your decision and I'd respect your view. I'd be honest though: I'm worried enough about your safety that if things got worse, we might have to think about the Mental Health Act. I'd much rather we agree this together.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just feel she'd be better off with someone else.\"","r":"\"That sounds such a painful thought to live with. It's the depression talking, and it's one of the reasons I'm worried. When you're better, most mums find that feeling goes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I still breastfeed there?\"","r":"\"Yes. The staff will help you feed her, and you can sleep while they settle her between feeds if you express. The pharmacist checks your medicine is fine for feeding.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg to 100 mg once daily in the morning from today; compatible with breastfeeding; review mood and side effects weekly","Admission bloods: FBC, ferritin, B12, folate, TFTs, U&E, LFT, glucose, CRP","Weigh her on admission and weekly; food and fluid chart for 72 hours","No routine hypnotic: protect sleep with night-feed support first; any hypnotic is agreed with the MBU pharmacist because she is breastfeeding"],"psych":["Named MBU nurse to agree a sleep plan: nursery nurse covers night feeds with expressed milk for two nights","Mother-infant interaction work with the MBU nursery nurse and psychologist once mood lifts","CBT for depression with the perinatal psychologist when concentration allows"],"social":["CPN to escort Amira and Lina by car at 15:00; husband Karim told by phone with her consent","MBU social worker to help with travel costs for Karim's evening visits","Health visitor, GP and community midwife informed of admission by the perinatal team today"],"safety":["Within-eyesight observation until transfer; she is not left alone with Lina before admission","MBU admission risk assessment on arrival, then nursing observation level set by the MBU team","If she withdraws consent before transfer: senior review and consider MHA assessment"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three perinatal red flags","x":"Recent big change in mental state, new thoughts or acts of violent self-harm, and new persistent feelings of failing as a mother or being estranged from the baby. Any one needs urgent senior review."},{"h":"MBU is the default","x":"NICE: a woman needing admission within 12 months of birth should normally go to a mother and baby unit, unless there are specific reasons not to."},{"h":"Sertraline 50 to 200 mg","x":"Start 50 mg in the morning, increase in 50 mg steps no faster than weekly. Low levels in breast milk; usually the first choice when breastfeeding."},{"h":"Not a safeguarding referral","x":"Admission is health care, not a child protection action. Say so plainly; fear of losing the baby is the commonest reason women refuse help."},{"h":"Ask about harm to the baby","x":"Ask directly about thoughts of harming the baby. Intrusive unwanted thoughts are common and frightening; thoughts she agrees with or plans are not."}],"sim":{"setup":"Thursday 11:00, home visit. Amira Haddad, 29, is six weeks after her first baby and severely depressed despite a week of crisis team visits. Your consultant has found an MBU bed. Recheck risk, explain the admission and agree it with her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Amira Haddad, 29, mother of six-week-old Lina","open":"The crisis nurse said you'd come. I know I'm not getting better. I'm sorry.","manner":"Slow, quiet and tearful; apologises a lot. Becomes anxious and briefly resistant when the unit is mentioned; settles when her fears are answered one by one and she feels it is her choice.","free":["She sleeps about two hours a night even when Lina sleeps","She has stopped eating properly and has lost weight","She feels Lina would be better off with someone else, like her mother-in-law","She feels like a failure as a mother"],"ifasked":["Last week, crossing the road, she thought about stepping in front of a bus; she stepped back","She sometimes wishes she would not wake up; no plan today, and she would not leave Lina","She has no thoughts of harming Lina","She has not heard voices; she does not think Lina is ill or damaged","She is breastfeeding and worried about her milk","Karim is at work and does not know how bad it is"],"worries":["Social services will take Lina away","A proper mum wouldn't need to go to hospital","Can Karim visit, and how far away is it?","What happens if I say no?"],"rules":["Cries when talking about Lina being better off without her; settles if the doctor names it as the illness","Initially says no to admission; agrees only after the social services worry and Karim's visits are answered","Asks 'what if I say no?' and only trusts the doctor if answered honestly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and asks how she has been since the crisis team started","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and the recent bus incident","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about thoughts of harming the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for psychotic features such as voices or beliefs about the baby","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and distress, naming them as part of the illness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why admission is recommended now: worsening despite home treatment, and risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the MBU concretely: baby stays, night support, feeding support, visiting","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers the fear about social services honestly and accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers 'what if I say no' honestly, including the possibility of the MHA","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees medication change and physical checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees practical arrangements: time, transport, packing, and telling her husband","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and confirms her consent freely given","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Risk rechecked including to the baby, admission explained concretely, fears answered honestly, and a freely agreed practical plan.","borderline":"Admission agreed but risk to the baby not asked, or MBU explained vaguely.","fail":"No risk check, coercive or misleading reassurance, or avoiding the social services and 'what if I say no' questions."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Amira. I'm Dr Rahman from the perinatal team. Thank you for letting us in. How have the last few days been since the crisis team started coming?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"The crisis nurse said you'd come. I know I'm not getting better. I'm sorry."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"You don't need to apologise. You're unwell, and it's our job to help. Tell me what's been hardest.","m":[5]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"I can't sleep, even when she sleeps. Two hours maybe. I don't eat. I just feel she'd be better off with someone else. Her grandmother is better with her than me."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"That sounds such a painful thought to live with. It's very common in depression after a baby, and it's the illness talking, not the truth about you.","m":[5]},{"t":"0:57","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When you feel like that, do you ever have thoughts about ending your life, or of not waking up?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I wish I wouldn't wake up. Last week, crossing the road, I thought about the bus. Then I stepped back."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That must have been frightening. Have you had thoughts like that since, or made any plans?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"No plans. I wouldn't leave Lina. But the thoughts come."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"And have you had any thoughts of harming Lina, even thoughts that scare you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I just feel I'm not good for her."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Have you heard voices, or felt that Lina is ill or damaged in some way?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"1:58","band":"Explain why","w":"D","s":"Amira, I'm going to be honest with you. The team has come twice a day for a week, and you're feeling worse, not better. With the thoughts about the bus, I'm worried about your safety.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"I think you and Lina need more help than we can give at home. I'd like you both to go to a mother and baby unit.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"A hospital? No. I can't. They'll say I'm a bad mother."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"I can see that's a big fear. Can I tell you what it's actually like, and then you tell me what worries you?","m":[5,7]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"2:46","band":"What the unit is","w":"D","s":"It's a small ward just for mums and babies. Lina sleeps in a cot beside your bed. There are nurses and nursery nurses there day and night.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"At night, if you express milk, they can do a feed so you can sleep. Sleep is one of the things that will help you get better.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"Can I keep breastfeeding?"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Yes. They'll help you with feeding, and the pharmacist checks every medicine is fine for Lina.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"Where is it?"},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"It's about fifty miles away. Karim can visit every evening, and weekends are flexible. The social worker can help with his travel costs.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:37","band":"Fears","w":"P","s":"If I go in, social services will be involved. They'll take her off me."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd worry about that. Going to the unit is about getting you well with Lina beside you. It isn't a referral to social services. We tell the health visitor and GP, because they look after you both.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"And a proper mum wouldn't need to go to hospital."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Depression after a baby is an illness. It's not a test of being a good mum. You've let us help, and that's what a good mum does.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"What if I say no?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"It's your decision, and I'd listen to why. I'll be honest, though. I'm worried enough that if things got worse, we might have to think about the Mental Health Act. I'd much rather we decide this together, today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"You'd section me?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Only if things got much worse and you couldn't stay safe. That's not where we are. Right now, I'm asking you.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"How long would I be there?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"It varies. Often a few weeks. We'd review it with you every week, and you'd go home when you and the team feel ready.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"We'll also increase your sertraline from 50 to 100 milligrams, starting today. It's fine with breastfeeding. And they'll do some blood tests, including thyroid and iron, because they can drag your mood down too.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"Do you think you could go today, with Lina?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Can Karim know first? He doesn't know how bad it's been."},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"Of course. Would you like to ring him now, or shall I speak to him with you?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Can you? I don't think I can say it."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I'll do it with you here. Then Lucy, the nurse, can drive you both at three. Your mother-in-law can help pack: Lina's things, your clothes, chargers, and anything that comforts you.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Before we ring him, can you tell me in your words what's going to happen?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Lina and I go to the unit today. They help at night so I can sleep. It's not social services. Karim can come in the evenings."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. And is this something you're agreeing to, not something you feel pushed into?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:38","w":"P","s":"I'm scared. But yes. I want to feel like her mum again."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's a brave decision, and I think it's the right one. Until you go, Lucy will stay with you. Let's ring Karim.","m":[12,5]}]}},
{"id":"c-p-09","n":1009,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"A father's postnatal depression three months after the birth: assessment and plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00","where":"Community mental health team assessment clinic","text":"Mr Kwame Mensah, 35, is referred by his GP with two months of low mood. His daughter, Ama, was born three months ago by emergency caesarean section. His GP letter says he is 'irritable, not sleeping, working late and drinking more'. His partner, Efua, 32, is recovering well. He arrives on his own, early, and apologises for 'wasting your time'."},"task":"Take a history to a diagnosis, assess risk to himself and to the baby, and agree a plan with treatment and support.","first60":["Read the GP letter for past depression, medicines and alcohol.","Plan to ask about the birth: an emergency caesarean can be traumatic for partners too.","Prepare to ask, in a calm way, about anger and coping with crying, as part of every new-parent assessment.","Have the PHQ-9 and AUDIT-C to hand, plus local NHS Talking Therapies details.","Remind yourself that fathers rarely volunteer how bad it is."],"ask":["'You said you're wasting my time. What made you go to the GP?'","'How's your mood been since Ama was born? When did it change?'","'How was the birth for you?'","'How are you sleeping when Ama is asleep?'","'How much are you drinking in a typical week?'","'When Ama cries and won't settle, how do you feel? What do you do?'","'Have you had thoughts of self-harm or that the family would be better off without you?'","'How is Efua doing in her mood?'"],"recognise":["Around 1 in 10 fathers have depression in the year after a birth; the risk is higher if the mother is depressed, money or work is strained, or he has had depression before.","Men often present with irritability, anger, withdrawal into work, alcohol and physical complaints rather than sadness.","A traumatic birth can cause intrusive memories and avoidance in partners; screen for PTSD symptoms.","Sleep loss from a baby is expected; poor sleep when the baby sleeps, early waking and loss of interest point to depression.","Ask about anger towards the baby; putting the baby down safely and walking away is a protective skill to praise.","Check the mother's mental health: paternal and maternal depression often occur together."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose a moderate depressive episode, assess risk to self and baby, start sertraline if he agrees, refer to NHS Talking Therapies, cut alcohol, and involve the GP and, with consent, the health visitor.","r":"Correct: this follows NICE depression guidance, treats him as a parent in the perinatal period and keeps the baby safe.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure him that new fathers are tired and suggest he takes a holiday.","r":"Unsafe: it misses a treatable depression and the risks of drinking and anger at home.","best":false},{"c":"Make a children's social care referral because he shouted when the baby cried.","r":"Not proportionate here: he put her down safely and walked away. Share with the health visitor with consent; refer only if risk to the baby emerges.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: share with the GP as routine; share with the health visitor with his consent. If you believe Ama is at risk of significant harm, share without consent and explain why (Working Together to Safeguard Children 2023).","NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) applies; NICE CG192 is written for women but its principles of early, family-aware care apply to fathers.","Fitness to drive: depression alone does not usually need DVLA notification unless symptoms affect safe driving; alcohol misuse may need notification."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're not wasting my time. What you're describing sounds like depression. It's common in dads after a baby, more than people realise, and it gets better with treatment."},{"to":"Patient (anger)","s":"Putting Ama in her cot and walking out when you felt angry was exactly the right thing to do. Lots of parents feel that rage at 3 a.m. What matters is what you did with it."},{"to":"Patient (plan)","s":"I'd suggest two things together: talking therapy and an antidepressant called sertraline. And cutting the evening drinking, which is making the sleep and mood worse."}],"write":"14:00 New assessment. Kwame Mensah 35M, GP referral. Daughter Ama 3/12, emergency CS.\nHx: 8/52 low mood, anhedonia, irritability, initial and early waking (wakes 04:00 when baby asleep), poor concentration at work, guilt. Intrusive images of the birth, no avoidance. Alcohol 4 cans 4.5% nightly (about 50 units/week), AUDIT-C 9. Past depression at 20, sertraline helped.\nRisk: passive thoughts 'family better off without me', no plan or intent; protective: Ama, Efua, faith. One episode of shouting at Ama at 03:00; placed her in cot and left room. No thoughts of harming her. Efua's mood good per Kwame.\nMSE: tired, low, reactive, no psychosis.\nA: Moderate depressive episode (ICD-11 6A70), alcohol use above low-risk limits.\nP: Sertraline 50 mg mane. Self-referral to NHS Talking Therapies today. Alcohol: aim 0-2 nights/week, AUDIT review. GP letter. HV informed with consent. Review 2 weeks. Crisis numbers given.\nDr S. Rahman CT2","make":["Open with his own words: what made him go to the GP.","Cover core depression symptoms, biological symptoms separate from baby-related sleep loss, and duration.","Ask about the birth, alcohol (units), past episodes and family history.","Ask about anger and coping with crying in a normalising way, then about thoughts of harming the baby.","Do a full suicide risk enquiry with protective factors.","Agree a plan in two or three steps with dates, and check he can say it back."],"next":"Kwame starts sertraline and self-refers to Talking Therapies the same afternoon. He tells Efua that evening. At review two weeks later he has cut to two drinks at weekends and is sleeping better; his low mood starts to lift by week four.","marks":["Took him seriously and normalised paternal depression.","Separated depressive sleep loss from baby-related waking.","Quantified alcohol in units.","Asked about anger and thoughts of harming the baby without judgement.","Completed a suicide risk enquiry with protective factors.","Agreed medication, therapy and alcohol plan with a review date."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'I'm just tired' at face value.","Not asking about thoughts of harm to the baby because he is the father.","Over-reacting to normal anger that was managed safely.","Forgetting to ask how the mother is."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022): treatment and management (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: postnatal depression in fathers and partners (checked Nov 2026)","5. ICON: Babies cry, you can cope (iconcope.org; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, fathers' mental health after a birth is recognised and treated. Ask about the baby and the partner in every new-parent assessment, and offer support to the man in front of you, not just the mother.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Diagnose his depression, assess risk to himself and the baby, and agree medication, therapy and review.","obj":["Established core depressive symptoms, duration and severity, separating depressive sleep loss from baby-related waking","Quantified alcohol use in units and asked about the birth and past depression","Asked about anger, coping with crying and thoughts of harming the baby, and praised safe coping","Completed a suicide risk enquiry with protective factors","Agreed sertraline 50 mg, NHS Talking Therapies, an alcohol goal and a review in 2 weeks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm probably wasting your time. All new dads are knackered.\"","r":"\"You're not wasting my time. Tiredness is normal; what you've told me about mood, sleep and drinking is more than that. Let's look at it properly.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Efua had the operation. I've got no right to feel like this.\"","r":"\"Her recovery matters, and so do you. Watching an emergency birth is frightening for partners too. Feeling low isn't taking anything away from her.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I shouted at her at three in the morning. A baby. What kind of dad does that?\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. Lots of parents feel that rage at night. You put her in her cot and walked out. That was the right thing to do. Can we talk about it?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want tablets. I'll just push through.\"","r":"\"That's your choice, and therapy alone is an option. Pushing through has been going on for two months, though. Sertraline helped you before. Could we try it for six weeks and review?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will they think I'm a danger to the baby if I tell the health visitor?\"","r":"\"No. Telling her that you're getting help for depression is a strength. If I ever thought Ama wasn't safe, I'd tell you myself before anything else.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The drinking is the only thing that helps me switch off.\"","r":"\"It feels like it helps at first. Then it wakes you at four and makes the mood worse the next day. Could we aim for no drinking on work nights?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily in the morning; review at 2 weeks; increase in 50 mg steps to 100 mg if partial response at 4 to 6 weeks; continue at least 6 months after recovery","Warn: nausea, loose stools, headache and early agitation in the first 2 weeks; sexual side effects; do not stop suddenly","Alcohol: from about 50 units a week to no drinking on work nights and under 14 units a week; check for withdrawal (morning shakes, sweats) before cutting down; repeat AUDIT-C at review","Bloods via GP: FBC, LFT, GGT, TFTs"],"psych":["Self-referral to NHS Talking Therapies today: CBT for depression, about 12 to 16 sessions with a high-intensity therapist","Ask Talking Therapies to screen for birth-related PTSD symptoms","Sleep plan agreed with Efua: alternate night feeds, one protected night each a week"],"social":["Health visitor informed with his consent; she can support both parents and check Efua's mood","GP fit note for two weeks if needed; consider occupational health and a phased return","Local dads' peer support group details and the NHS website page on fathers' mental health"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 mental health option, Samaritans 116 123, and 999 if he feels he may act","Coping with crying: put Ama safely in her cot, walk away, breathe, ask Efua or a friend to take over (ICON message)","GP review in 2 weeks; CMHT review if worse, if suicidal thoughts become active, or if any thought of harming Ama appears"]},"facts":[{"h":"About 1 in 10 fathers","x":"Paternal depression after a birth is common and peaks around 3 to 6 months. It is more likely if the mother is depressed or he has had depression before."},{"h":"Men present differently","x":"Irritability, anger, long hours at work, drinking and physical symptoms often replace obvious sadness. Ask about them directly."},{"h":"Sleep when the baby sleeps","x":"Waking because the baby cries is normal. Lying awake while the baby sleeps, or waking early, points to depression."},{"h":"Praise safe coping","x":"Putting a crying baby in the cot and walking away is the ICON advice. Saying so lowers shame and makes honest disclosure more likely."},{"h":"Ask how the mother is","x":"Depression in one parent raises the risk in the other. One question about her mood can prevent a second missed diagnosis."}],"sim":{"setup":"Tuesday 14:00, CMHT assessment clinic. Kwame Mensah, 35, is referred by his GP with low mood since his daughter was born three months ago. Take a history, assess risk to himself and the baby, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Kwame Mensah, 35, father of three-month-old Ama","open":"Thanks. I'm probably wasting your time. All new dads are knackered, aren't they?","manner":"Polite, guarded and self-critical; plays things down at first. Opens up when not judged, and becomes tearful when talking about shouting at the baby. Relieved and practical once he hears a plan.","free":["Ama was born by emergency caesarean section after a long labour","He is tired all the time and irritable at work and home","He has been staying late at work rather than going home","He feels guilty because Efua had the operation, not him"],"ifasked":["Mood low most days for about two months; nothing feels enjoyable, including football","He lies awake when Ama is asleep and wakes at 4 a.m.","Four cans of 4.5% lager most nights","He had depression at 20; sertraline helped","He has images of the birth, the alarm and the theatre, that pop into his head","Once at 3 a.m. he shouted at Ama, put her in her cot and walked out for five minutes","Sometimes thinks the family would be better off without him; no plans; his faith and Ama stop him","Efua seems fine and is sleeping better than him"],"worries":["Will the health visitor think I'm a danger to Ama?","I don't want tablets","Will my work find out?"],"rules":["Says 'I'm fine, just tired' unless asked specific questions","Only discloses the shouting episode if asked about crying or anger in a non-judgemental way","Becomes tearful about the shouting; settles if the safe action is praised","Accepts sertraline if reminded it helped before and given a review date"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and responds to 'wasting your time' with reassurance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes low mood, anhedonia and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Separates depressive sleep problems from baby-related waking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the birth and screens for intrusive memories","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies alcohol in units and asks about past depression and treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about anger and coping with crying, and about thoughts of harming the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his guilt and distress and praises safe coping","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about the partner's mental health","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the diagnosis of depression in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees sertraline with dose, side effects and review, and Talking Therapies referral","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees an alcohol goal, explains information sharing with the health visitor, and gives a crisis plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and summarises next steps with dates","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear diagnosis of depression, risk to self and baby assessed without judgement, and a dated plan he can repeat back.","borderline":"Depression identified but alcohol not quantified, or baby risk asked awkwardly or not at all.","fail":"Dismissed as tiredness, no risk enquiry, or a judgemental reaction to his anger that shuts down disclosure."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Mensah. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the psychiatry doctors. Your GP asked us to see you. Is Kwame OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Kwame's fine. Thanks. I'm probably wasting your time. All new dads are knackered, aren't they?"},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"You're not wasting my time at all. Being tired with a baby is normal, but your GP was worried about more than that. What made you go?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Efua made me. She says I'm snappy all the time. I stay late at work so I don't have to go home. That's not like me."},{"t":"0:44","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been since Ama was born?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Low, I suppose. Most days for a couple of months. Nothing's fun. I used to love football. I don't even watch it now."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"When Ama's asleep, are you able to sleep?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"No. That's the stupid thing. She sleeps and I lie there. And I wake at four, every day."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"That's an important difference. Waking because a baby cries is normal; lying awake when she's asleep, and waking early, often points to depression.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"How was the birth for you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Horrible. The alarm went off, everyone ran, they took her to theatre. I sat in a corridor thinking they'd both die."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. Do pictures of that day come back when you don't want them to?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. The alarm, mostly. But Efua had the operation, not me. I've got no right to feel like this."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Her recovery matters, and so do you. Partners can be badly shaken by an emergency birth too. Feeling low doesn't take anything away from her.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:12","band":"Drinking and past","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking in a typical week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Few cans at night. Four, maybe. It helps me switch off."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Four cans of normal-strength lager every night is about fifty units a week, more than three times the low-risk limit. Has anything like this happened before?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"At uni. I was on sertraline for a year. It worked."},{"t":"2:43","band":"Risk to the baby","w":"D","s":"Lots of parents tell me that when a baby cries at 3 a.m. and won't settle, they feel real anger. How is that for you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"Once I shouted at her. Really shouted. Then I put her in her cot and walked out. What kind of dad does that?"},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that took courage. You put her down safely and walked away. That's exactly the right thing to do.","m":[8,6]},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had thoughts of harming her, or worried that you might?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd never touch her. I just hated myself."},{"t":"3:32","band":"Risk to self","w":"D","s":"When you feel that low, do you have thoughts of self-harm or that the family would be better off without you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think they'd be better off without me. I haven't thought about doing anything."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"What stops you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Ama. And my faith. I'd never leave her."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Those are strong reasons. If those thoughts got stronger, or you started to think of a way, I'd need you to tell someone straight away.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"How is Efua in her mood?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"She's good, honestly. Better than me."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Kwame, what you're describing sounds like depression: low mood, losing interest, poor sleep and guilt for two months. It happens to about one in ten dads after a baby. It's treatable.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"So I'm not just weak."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Not at all. It's an illness, and you've come for help. That's the opposite of weak.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:47","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest a few things. First, talking therapy through NHS Talking Therapies. You can refer yourself online today.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Second, sertraline again, as it helped before. Fifty milligrams in the morning. The first two weeks can bring a bit of nausea or jitteriness; it usually settles.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"I don't really want tablets."},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"That's fair, and therapy alone is an option. You've pushed through for two months, though. Could we try it for six weeks and review together?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"OK. Six weeks."},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"Third, the drinking. It wakes you at four and drags your mood down. Could we aim for no drinking on work nights to start?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I can try that."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"I'd also like to let your health visitor know you're getting help, with your agreement. It's so she can support you both. It's not because I think Ama is unsafe.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"As long as it's not social services."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"It isn't. If I ever had that worry, I'd tell you first. If you feel unsafe, ring 111 and choose the mental health option, or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:17","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"Sertraline in the morning, refer myself for therapy today, no beers on work nights, and you'll tell the health visitor."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Your GP will see you in two weeks. Is there anything I haven't covered?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. I feel better for saying it out loud."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you did. Go home to Efua and Ama. You'll get through this.","m":[13]}]}},
{"id":"c-p-10","n":1010,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🕯","title":"Stillbirth at 38 weeks: a grief review six weeks later","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 10:30","where":"Maternal mental health service clinic, women's hospital","text":"Ms Sophie Turner, 31, gave birth to her son, Leo, stillborn at 38 weeks, six weeks ago. It was her first pregnancy. The bereavement midwife has referred her because she 'cries every day and isn't sleeping' and asked whether she is depressed. The post-mortem results are not back yet. Her partner, Ben, 33, is in the waiting room."},"task":"Assess whether this is grief or depression (or trauma), assess risk, and agree support with her.","first60":["Read the referral and the maternity notes: the birth, any complications, and what she has been told about the cause.","Use his name, Leo, from the start; check the notes for how she refers to him.","Make sure the room has no baby posters or leaflets and that you will not be interrupted.","Have Sands details and the bereavement midwife's direct number ready.","Ask her first whether she would like Ben to join, now or later."],"ask":["'Would you like to tell me about Leo?'","'How have the weeks since he was born been for you?'","'Are there moments, even small ones, when the pain eases or you can enjoy something?'","'How do you feel about yourself as a person?'","'Do pictures of the birth come back when you don't want them to? Nightmares?'","'Some parents tell me they wish they could be with their baby. Have you had thoughts like that?'","'Have you thought of ending your life or made any plans?'","'Who is supporting you? How is Ben?'"],"recognise":["Grief comes in waves of longing and sadness triggered by reminders, with moments of relief; self-esteem is mostly preserved, and guilt focuses on the loss.","Depression is more pervasive: persistent low mood most of the day, loss of interest in everything, worthlessness, hopelessness, and marked slowing or weight change.","A wish to be with the baby is common in grief; separate it from active suicidal thinking, plans or intent.","Traumatic birth and stillbirth can cause PTSD: intrusions, nightmares, avoidance and hyperarousal; screen again at 3 months.","Prolonged grief disorder (ICD-11) needs at least 6 months after the death and marked impairment; it cannot be diagnosed at 6 weeks.","Anger at the hospital is common; listen, do not defend, and point to the review process."],"decide":[{"c":"Recognise acute grief with trauma symptoms but no depressive episode now; assess risk; offer continued bereavement midwife support, Sands, optional counselling, and review in 4 weeks with clear signs to come back sooner.","r":"Correct: it avoids labelling normal grief as illness while keeping watch for depression, PTSD and risk.","best":true},{"c":"Start sertraline because she cries every day.","r":"Wrong: crying and sadness at six weeks after a stillbirth are expected. Medicine is for a depressive episode, not for grief.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her she should be starting to move on by now.","r":"Harmful: there is no timetable for grief. It shuts down disclosure and damages trust.","best":false}],"law":["A stillbirth (a baby born dead after 24 weeks) must be registered within 42 days in England and Wales; the bereavement midwife usually helps with this.","Parents of a stillborn baby keep full statutory maternity leave and pay, and the partner keeps paternity leave and pay.","Every stillbirth is reviewed with the Perinatal Mortality Review Tool (PMRT); parents are invited to give their questions and see the findings.","Concerns about care can go to PALS or a formal complaint; the trust has a duty of candour where harm was caused by care."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm so sorry about Leo. There's no right way to feel after losing him. I'd like to understand how you're doing, so we can make sure you get the right support."},{"to":"Patient (explaining)","s":"From what you've told me, this sounds like grief: deep, painful and normal after losing Leo. I don't think you're depressed right now, but I want to keep an eye on that with you."},{"to":"Patient (questions about care)","s":"Your questions about what happened matter. The hospital reviews every stillbirth, and you can send your questions to be part of that. I'll help you do that."}],"write":"10:30 MMHS assessment. Sophie Turner 31F. Stillbirth of son Leo at 38/40, 6 weeks ago, first pregnancy. PM results awaited. Ref: bereavement midwife.\nHx: cries daily in waves, triggered by reminders; sleeps 5-6 h; eating OK; enjoyed a walk with sister; self-worth intact apart from guilt 'my body failed him'. Intrusive images of silent delivery room 3-4x/week, nightmares 1x/week, avoids nursery. Angry that reduced movements were 'dismissed' 2 days before.\nRisk: wishes at times she 'could be with Leo'; no active suicidal thoughts, no plan or intent. Protective: Ben, mother, hope of another pregnancy. No self-harm. Alcohol 2-3 units/week.\nMSE: tearful, reactive, warm rapport, no psychosis.\nA: Acute grief with post-traumatic stress symptoms. No current depressive episode. Low risk to self.\nP: Bereavement midwife weekly; Sands details given; Talking Therapies offered (declined for now). PMRT questions via bereavement midwife. PALS details given. Review 4 weeks, PTSD screen then. Crisis plan given. GP letter.\nDr S. Rahman CT2","make":["Say his name and invite her to talk about him; let silences happen.","Ask about moments of relief or enjoyment, and about self-worth: these separate grief from depression.","Screen for trauma: intrusions, nightmares, avoidance, being on edge.","Ask about the wish to be with the baby openly, then clarify active thoughts, plans and intent.","Listen to anger about care without defending; point to the PMRT and PALS.","Agree support and a review date, with clear signs to come back sooner."],"next":"Sophie sends her questions about the reduced movements to the PMRT via the bereavement midwife. She and Ben go to a Sands group. At review four weeks later, the intrusions are less frequent; she agrees to trauma-focused CBT after the post-mortem results meeting.","marks":["Used Leo's name and let her tell her story.","Separated grief from depression using enjoyment, self-worth and pervasiveness.","Screened for trauma symptoms.","Clarified the wish to be with Leo versus active suicidal thinking.","Responded to anger about care without defending, pointing to the review process.","Agreed support and review, avoiding unnecessary medication."],"pitfalls":["Referring to 'the baby' or 'the pregnancy' instead of his name.","Medicalising normal grief with an antidepressant.","Skipping the risk question because it feels intrusive.","Defending the maternity team or speculating about the cause."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. National Bereavement Care Pathway (NBCP): stillbirth pathway (checked Nov 2026)","3. Sands: stillbirth and neonatal death charity (sands.org.uk; checked Nov 2026)","4. WHO ICD-11: prolonged grief disorder (6B42) (checked Nov 2026)","5. Perinatal Mortality Review Tool (PMRT), MBRRACE-UK (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK parents usually name a stillborn baby, hold him and keep photos and footprints. Use his name and ask about him; avoiding it can feel to parents as if their baby did not exist.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"Separate grief from depression and trauma, assess suicide risk, and agree support with a 4-week review.","obj":["Used the baby's name and invited her to talk about him and the birth","Distinguished grief from depression by asking about enjoyment, self-worth and how pervasive the low mood is","Screened for post-traumatic symptoms: intrusions, nightmares and avoidance","Clarified a wish to be with the baby versus active suicidal thoughts, plans and intent","Agreed support (bereavement midwife, Sands, optional therapy) and a review in 4 weeks with signs to return sooner"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone wants me to be getting better. It's only been six weeks.\"","r":"\"Six weeks is no time at all. There's no timetable for losing Leo. I'm not here to hurry you, just to make sure you have the right support.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My body failed him. It's my fault.\"","r":"\"I hear how much you're blaming yourself. We don't know the cause yet. When the results come, the consultant will go through them with you. Can we look at what's helping you carry this?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I told them he wasn't moving as much. They sent me home.\"","r":"\"That sounds incredibly painful to live with. Your questions matter. The hospital reviews every stillbirth, and you can send your questions to be part of it. I'll help you do that.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I just want to be with him.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Many parents feel that. Can I ask more? Have you had thoughts about ending your life, or made any plans?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I depressed? Should I be on tablets?\"","r":"\"From what you've told me, this sounds like grief, which is painful but normal. Tablets don't treat grief. If it turns into depression, we'd talk about treatment, and I'll keep checking with you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do I tell people at work?\"","r":"\"You choose how much to say. Some parents ask a manager to tell the team before they go back. You still have your full maternity leave, so there's no rush.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No antidepressant now: no depressive episode. Reassess at 4 weeks with PHQ-9 and a PTSD screen","GP to check physical recovery: bleeding, wound or perineum, contraception wishes, and iron if she was anaemic","Advise against using alcohol or sleeping tablets to sleep; simple sleep routine and daylight walks"],"psych":["Continued weekly contact with the bereavement midwife until the post-mortem results meeting","Offer counselling or NHS Talking Therapies now (her choice); trauma-focused CBT or EMDR if PTSD symptoms persist at 3 months","Offer the same support to Ben, separately or as a couple"],"social":["Sands: helpline, online community and local parent groups, for her and Ben","Bereavement midwife to support PMRT questions and the post-mortem results appointment with the consultant obstetrician","PALS details if she wants to raise concerns about care","Maternity leave and pay continue in full; GP fit note if needed after that"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 mental health option, Samaritans 116 123, and contact the clinic if the wish to be with Leo becomes a thought of ending her life","Expect hard days: Leo's due date, the results meeting and anniversaries; plan support for each","Review in 4 weeks; GP informed by letter today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Grief keeps self-worth","x":"In grief the world feels empty; in depression the self feels worthless. Waves, moments of relief and preserved self-esteem point to grief."},{"h":"Prolonged grief needs 6 months","x":"ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder needs persistent longing or preoccupation for at least 6 months, with marked impairment. Do not diagnose it at 6 weeks."},{"h":"Register within 42 days","x":"A stillbirth after 24 weeks must be registered within 42 days in England and Wales. The bereavement midwife usually helps parents with this."},{"h":"Parents can shape the review","x":"Every stillbirth is reviewed with the PMRT. Parents are invited to send questions and receive the findings; tell them how."},{"h":"A wish to be with the baby","x":"Common in grief. Ask openly, then clarify any active thoughts, plans and intent. Do not assume it is suicidal, and do not ignore it."}],"sim":{"setup":"Monday 10:30, maternal mental health clinic. Sophie Turner, 31, gave birth to her son Leo, stillborn at 38 weeks, six weeks ago. Her bereavement midwife asks whether she is depressed. Assess grief, depression and risk, and agree support.","role":{"who":"Ms Sophie Turner, 31, Leo's mother","open":"The midwife thinks I'm depressed. I don't know what I am. I just miss him.","manner":"Quiet, tearful and warm; brightens when talking about Leo. Becomes angry and sharp about being sent home with reduced movements; calms if the doctor listens without defending the hospital.","free":["She cries every day, in waves, especially when she sees prams or the nursery door","She held Leo and has photos and his footprints","She feels her body failed him","The post-mortem results are not back yet"],"ifasked":["She enjoyed a walk with her sister last week and laughed once, then felt guilty","She sleeps 5 to 6 hours and is eating, though less","She feels she is still a good person and a good partner","She sees the silent delivery room in her head three or four times a week and has nightmares weekly","She avoids the nursery and the road to the hospital","She reported reduced movements two days before and was sent home after a normal trace","Sometimes she wishes she could be with Leo; no thoughts of ending her life, no plans","Ben is struggling too but 'won't talk'"],"worries":["Am I depressed? Should I be on tablets?","Everyone expects me to be better by now","Will anyone listen about the reduced movements?"],"rules":["Softens noticeably if the doctor uses Leo's name","Gets angry about being sent home; settles if listened to and given the review route, escalates if the doctor defends staff","Only mentions the wish to be with Leo if asked sensitively about such thoughts"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, offers condolences and uses Leo's name","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Invites her to talk about Leo and what has happened since","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about moments of enjoyment and relief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-worth, sleep and appetite to separate grief from depression","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for intrusions, nightmares and avoidance","d":"Content"},{"c":"Listens to anger about care without defending, and explains the PMRT and PALS","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about a wish to be with Leo, then active suicidal thoughts, plans and intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about protective factors and support, including her partner","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains that this sounds like grief, not depression, and why no medication now","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees support: bereavement midwife, Sands, optional therapy, support for her partner","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a crisis plan, signs to return sooner and a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Warm, unhurried, uses Leo's name, separates grief from depression on evidence, clarifies risk and agrees support with a review.","borderline":"Kind but misses the trauma screen or does not clarify the wish to be with Leo.","fail":"Labels grief as depression and starts medication, defends the hospital, or omits risk."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Sophie. I'm Dr Rahman. I'm so sorry about Leo. Thank you for coming in today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"The midwife thinks I'm depressed. I don't know what I am. I just miss him."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Of course you miss him. I'd like to understand how you're doing, so we get the right support around you. Would you tell me about Leo?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"He was perfect. Dark hair like Ben. I held him for hours. We've got photos and his footprints."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"He sounds beautiful. I'm glad you had that time with him.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:43","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"How have the weeks since been for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"I cry every day. It comes in waves. Prams, the nursery door. Everyone wants me to be getting better. It's only been six weeks."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"Six weeks is no time at all. There's no timetable for losing Leo. I'm not here to hurry you.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:09","band":"Grief or depression","w":"D","s":"Are there moments, even small ones, when the pain eases, or you can enjoy something?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"I went for a walk with my sister last week. I laughed at something. Then I felt awful for laughing."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"That happens to a lot of parents. Laughing doesn't mean you love him any less.","m":[3,9]},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about yourself as a person?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"I'm still me, I think. A good partner. But my body failed him. That's what I can't get past."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you're blaming yourself. We don't know the cause yet, and the results will be gone through with you. How are you sleeping and eating?","m":[9,4]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Five or six hours. I eat, just less."},{"t":"2:06","band":"Trauma","w":"D","s":"Do pictures of the birth come back when you don't want them to, or in dreams?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"The silence. When he was born and nobody said anything. I see that three or four times a week. Nightmares too."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds very distressing. Do you find yourself avoiding things that remind you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"I don't go in the nursery. I drive the long way so I don't pass the hospital."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Anger about care","w":"P","s":"And the thing is, I told them two days before he wasn't moving as much. They did a trace and sent me home."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"That sounds incredibly painful to carry. Thank you for telling me.","m":[9,6]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Nobody's said if that mattered. Nobody's listened."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"I'm listening. I can't tell you whether it changed things, and I won't guess. The hospital reviews every stillbirth, and parents are invited to send their questions. Your bereavement midwife can help you send yours.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"If you want to raise it as a concern now, PALS can help too. I'll give you their details.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"OK. I want them to answer that."},{"t":"3:31","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Some parents tell me that they wish they could be with their baby. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. At night. I just want to be with him."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have those thoughts ever turned into thinking about ending your life, or making any plans?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"No. No plans. I'd never do that to Ben, or my mum. And I want to try again one day."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"Those are really important. Have you harmed yourself at all, or been drinking more?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"No. A glass of wine at the weekend."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Who's supporting you? And how is Ben?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"My mum, my sister. Ben's struggling but he won't talk."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Sophie, from what you've told me, this sounds like grief. It's deep and painful, and it's normal after losing Leo. You still have moments of relief, you feel like yourself, and you're sleeping and eating.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"That tells me you're not depressed right now. The pictures and nightmares are trauma symptoms, which are common after a birth like yours.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"So no tablets?"},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Tablets don't treat grief. If it turned into depression, or the trauma symptoms don't ease, we'd talk about treatment then.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:07","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest keeping your weekly contact with the bereavement midwife, and looking at Sands. They have a helpline and local groups for parents, and Ben too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"If you'd like to talk to a counsellor now, I can refer you. If the pictures and nightmares are still there in a couple of months, trauma-focused therapy works well.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"Maybe later. Could Ben get help?"},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Yes. He can come with you, or see someone on his own. Sands supports dads as well.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"D","s":"If the wish to be with Leo ever becomes a thought of ending your life, ring 111 and choose the mental health option, or Samaritans on 116 123, and let us know.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"The results meeting and Leo's due date may be hard days. Let's plan who'll be with you then.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"My mum. She's said she'll come."},{"t":"6:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'd like to see you again in four weeks. What will you take away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"That this is grief, not me going mad. Send my questions through the midwife. Sands for me and Ben. Ring if it gets darker."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Is there anything I haven't asked that you wanted to say?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Just that you said his name. Thank you."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Leo matters. I'll see you in four weeks.","m":[1]}]}},
{"id":"c-p-11","n":1011,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🛡","title":"Pre-birth safeguarding at 30 weeks: personality disorder, substance use and an honest conversation","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 14:00","where":"Perinatal mental health outpatient clinic","text":"Miss Chloe Barnes, 19, is 30 weeks pregnant with her first baby. She has a diagnosis of emotionally unstable personality disorder, smokes cannabis daily and has used cocaine at weekends. She cut her arms two months ago. She is a care leaver living in supported accommodation; her boyfriend, Ryan, 23, is known to police. Children's social care has started a pre-birth assessment, and an initial child protection conference is in three weeks. The social worker has asked for your report. Chloe arrives angry: 'You're all building a case to take my baby.'"},"task":"Assess current risks, explain the pre-birth assessment and conference honestly, tell her what you will report, and agree a plan that gives her and the baby the best chance.","first60":["Read the social worker's request, the midwife's notes and the last drug screen result.","Check whether Ryan is with her; plan to see her alone for part of the meeting to ask about domestic abuse.","Decide what you will put in your report, so you can be open with her about it.","Have her leaving-care personal adviser's details and the substance misuse midwife's number ready.","Remember she has a right to be heard: start with her view, not the agency concerns."],"ask":["'You sound really worried about this. What have you been told is happening?'","'What would you want the conference to know about you?'","'How much cannabis are you using now? Anything else, including alcohol and cocaine?'","'When did you last hurt yourself? How are you coping with urges now?'","'Has Ryan ever hurt you, frightened you or controlled what you do?'","'Have you had thoughts about ending your life?'","'Who supports you? Who could help with the baby?'"],"recognise":["Pre-birth risk factors: parental substance misuse, domestic abuse, self-harm, unstable housing, young age, the parent's own care history and limited support.","Strengths count too: engagement with midwives, insight, wanting help, reduction in drug use, and a supportive adult such as a personal adviser.","EUPD symptoms often intensify under threat of loss; anger may hide fear and shame. Stay calm, consistent and honest.","Cannabis in pregnancy is linked with lower birth weight; cocaine with placental abruption and preterm birth; tobacco with growth restriction.","Domestic abuse often starts or escalates in pregnancy; ask her alone.","Your role: assess and treat her mental health, give an honest, balanced report, and help her engage. You do not decide whether the baby goes home."],"decide":[{"c":"Validate her fear, explain the process and possible outcomes honestly, share what you will report (strengths and concerns), ask about domestic abuse alone, and agree a concrete plan she can show the conference.","r":"Correct: honesty and transparency build trust, give her a real chance to make changes, and keep the baby safe.","best":true},{"c":"Promise her that if she engages, the baby will definitely come home with her.","r":"Dishonest: you cannot control the outcome. Broken promises damage trust and her mental health.","best":false},{"c":"Avoid discussing the conference and send your report directly to the social worker.","r":"Wrong: parents should know what professionals are saying about them, and secrecy feeds mistrust and crisis.","best":false}],"law":["Children Act 1989 s47: children's social care must make enquiries where there is reasonable cause to suspect a child is suffering, or likely to suffer, significant harm; this can begin before birth.","Working Together to Safeguard Children 2023: parents are invited to child protection conferences, should see reports beforehand and can bring a supporter or advocate. Health professionals must share information and contribute to assessment.","Possible outcomes: no plan, child in need support, or a child protection plan; if concerns are serious, the local authority may start pre-proceedings under the Public Law Outline, and she should get a solicitor.","Care leavers are entitled to a personal adviser up to age 25 (Children and Social Work Act 2017).","Information sharing for safeguarding does not need consent where seeking it would place the child at risk, but tell her what you share unless that would increase risk."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can hear how frightened you are. I'm not here to build a case. My job is to help you, and to be honest with you, including about what I write."},{"to":"Patient (honesty)","s":"I can't promise what the conference will decide, and I won't pretend I can. What I can promise is that my report will be fair, it will include what's going well, and you'll see it first."},{"to":"Patient (what helps)","s":"What makes the biggest difference is what you do in the next three weeks: turning up, clear drug tests, keeping safe, and letting people help you."}],"write":"14:00 Perinatal clinic. Chloe Barnes 19F, 30/40, G1. EUPD. Care leaver, supported accommodation. Pre-birth assessment under way; ICPC in 3 weeks.\nSeen alone for part of the meeting.\nS: Very angry and fearful about the baby being removed. Cannabis now 2 joints/evening (down from all day); last cocaine 3 weeks ago; tobacco 10/day; alcohol nil. Last self-harm 2 months ago (superficial cuts), urges weekly, uses ice and calling PA. Fleeting suicidal thoughts when stressed, no plan or intent. Disclosed: Ryan pushed her into a wall 4 weeks ago, also checks her phone; no injuries.\nMSE: angry then tearful, reactive, engaged, no psychosis.\nA: EUPD with substance use in pregnancy; domestic abuse disclosed; moderate risk of self-harm under stress; risk to unborn baby from substances and DA.\nP: Explained conference, outcomes and my report; she will see it. DASH completed (score 9); IDVA referral with consent; social worker informed of DA disclosure, Chloe aware. Substance misuse midwife referral; weekly urine screen. DBT-informed skills group. NRT. Crisis plan. Review 1 week.\nDr S. Rahman CT2","make":["Start with her view and her fear; validate before giving any information.","Explain the process step by step: assessment, conference, who attends, possible outcomes.","Say what you will report: concerns and strengths, specifically. Offer to show her.","See her alone and ask about domestic abuse directly; complete a DASH risk checklist if she discloses.","Assess self-harm, suicide and substance use in detail.","Agree a short list of concrete actions she controls before the conference."],"next":"Chloe goes to the substance misuse midwife and her next two urine screens show only cannabis at falling levels. She brings her personal adviser to the conference. A child protection plan is made, with a parent and baby foster placement agreed as the first step after birth; she is upset but says she was not surprised, because she had been told honestly.","marks":["Validated her fear and anger before giving information.","Explained the conference and possible outcomes honestly, without promises.","Told her what the report will say, including strengths.","Asked about domestic abuse alone and acted on the disclosure.","Assessed self-harm, suicide and substance use specifically.","Agreed concrete actions she controls and a review date."],"pitfalls":["Making promises about the outcome.","Becoming defensive or punitive when she is angry.","Asking about domestic abuse with the partner present, or not at all.","Writing a report that lists only deficits."],"refs":["1. HM Government. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG78 Borderline personality disorder: recognition and management (2009; checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","5. SafeLives DASH risk identification checklist (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, parents see the reports written about them and attend child protection conferences. Write as if she will read every word, because she will.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Risk assessment + Breaking bad news","do":"Explain the pre-birth conference and your report honestly, assess risks including domestic abuse, and agree concrete actions.","obj":["Validated her fear and anger before explaining the pre-birth assessment and conference","Explained possible conference outcomes honestly without promising one","Told her what your report will say, including at least two strengths, and offered to show it to her","Assessed self-harm, suicide, substance use and domestic abuse, with her seen alone","Agreed three concrete actions she controls before the conference, and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You're all building a case to take my baby.\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightened you are. I'm not here to build a case. I'm here to help you, and to be honest with you about everything, including what I write.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I do everything you say, will I keep her?\"","r":"\"I wish I could promise that. I can't, and I won't lie to you. What I can say is that what you do now really counts, and I'll make sure it's in my report.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I was in care. I know how this ends.\"","r":"\"Your experience makes complete sense of how scared you are. Your story isn't decided yet. You've already cut down a lot, and that shows what you can do.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Ryan pushed me once. But he was stressed.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that was brave. Being pushed is not OK, whatever the stress. I'd like to ask a few more questions so we can keep you and the baby safe.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to that conference. They'll just judge me.\"","r":"\"It will be hard, I know. If you're there, they hear your side from you. You can bring your personal adviser, and you'll see the reports first. Would that make it more possible?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I want to cut right now. I can't do this.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me rather than hiding it. Let's stay with this for a minute. What helped last time the urge was this strong? Shall we ring Jade together?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No psychotropic medicine needed now; avoid benzodiazepines; do not start valproate in pregnancy","Nicotine replacement: 16-hour patch 15 mg plus inhalator, via the stop smoking in pregnancy service","Weekly urine drug screen with the substance misuse midwife; growth scans as the obstetric team plans","Self-harm wounds checked by GP or midwife; tetanus status checked"],"psych":["DBT-informed skills group or individual skills work with the perinatal psychologist, weekly, starting before birth","Crisis and distress-tolerance plan written with her: ice, cold water, call personal adviser, crisis line","Parent-infant relationship work booked for after birth (perinatal team and health visitor)"],"social":["Social worker leads the pre-birth assessment; your written report is shared with Chloe before the conference","IDVA referral with her consent; DASH completed; MARAC if the score reaches the threshold (14 or more, or professional judgement)","Personal adviser (leaving care team) to attend the conference with her and help with a solicitor if pre-proceedings start","Family group conference offered to identify any family support"],"safety":["Social worker told of the domestic abuse disclosure today; Chloe knows and agreed the wording","Crisis numbers: perinatal team in hours, 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if in danger","Review in 1 week, then fortnightly until birth; midwife and health visitor in the core group"]},"facts":[{"h":"Parents see the reports","x":"Under Working Together, parents are invited to conferences and should see reports in advance. Write factually, include strengths, and show her before you send it."},{"h":"Never promise the outcome","x":"You do not decide where the baby lives. Promises you cannot keep destroy trust; honest uncertainty with concrete actions keeps her engaged."},{"h":"Ask about abuse alone","x":"Domestic abuse often starts or worsens in pregnancy. Ask with the partner out of the room; use the DASH checklist and refer to an IDVA."},{"h":"Care leavers keep a personal adviser","x":"Care leavers are entitled to a personal adviser up to 25. Invite them; they are often the steadiest adult in her life."},{"h":"Strengths are evidence","x":"Reduced use, attendance, insight and support are real evidence. A balanced report is fairer and more useful to the conference."}],"sim":{"setup":"Friday 14:00, perinatal clinic. Chloe Barnes, 19, is 30 weeks pregnant, with EUPD and cannabis and cocaine use. A pre-birth child protection conference is in three weeks and the social worker wants your report. Assess risks and talk honestly with her.","role":{"who":"Miss Chloe Barnes, 19, pregnant at 30 weeks","open":"I know why I'm here. You're all building a case to take my baby.","manner":"Angry, defensive and quick to feel judged; swears once. Softens when her fear is named and when strengths are mentioned. Becomes tearful and has an urge to self-harm when outcomes are discussed; steadies with calm, practical support.","free":["She grew up in foster care from age 9","She has cut down cannabis from 'all day' to a couple of joints at night","She has been to every midwife appointment","She is terrified the baby will be taken at birth"],"ifasked":["Last cocaine three weeks ago, at a party","Last self-harm two months ago, superficial cuts; urges weekly; she uses ice and calls Jade, her personal adviser","Fleeting thoughts of being better off dead when stressed; no plan; the baby stops her","Ryan pushed her into a wall four weeks ago and checks her phone; she has not told anyone","Smokes 10 cigarettes a day; no alcohol","She does not know she can see the reports or bring someone to the conference"],"worries":["If I do everything you say, will I keep her?","What will you write about me?","I'm not going to that conference"],"rules":["Becomes more hostile if the doctor is defensive or lists her problems first","Only discloses Ryan's behaviour if seen alone and asked directly but gently","Asks for a promise she will keep the baby; trusts the doctor more if refused honestly","Says she wants to cut when outcomes are explained; settles with a calm, practical response"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms she is alone, and validates her fear and anger before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she understands and what she wants the conference to know","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the pre-birth assessment and conference in plain words, including her right to attend and bring support","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains possible outcomes honestly and refuses to promise one","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells her what the report will say, including strengths, and offers to show it first","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses substance use specifically: cannabis, cocaine, alcohol and tobacco","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses self-harm and suicidal thoughts, with coping and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about domestic abuse directly and responds safely to disclosure","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds calmly to the urge to self-harm and agrees immediate coping steps","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains what will be shared with the social worker and why","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees concrete actions she controls: drug service, screens, skills work, IDVA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and arranges review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Honest, calm and non-judgemental; explains the process and outcomes without promises, assesses all risks including domestic abuse, and agrees concrete steps.","borderline":"Process explained and risks assessed, but strengths missed or domestic abuse not explored.","fail":"Promises an outcome, becomes defensive or punitive, or ignores self-harm or domestic abuse."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, Chloe. I'm Dr Rahman from the perinatal team. Is it just you today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I know why I'm here. You're all building a case to take my baby."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightened you are. I'm not here to build a case. I'm here to help you, and to be honest with you, including about what I write.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"That's what they all say."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"That's fair, given what you've been through. Can you tell me what you've been told is happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Social worker came. She said there's a conference. Basically a meeting to decide if I'm a fit mum."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"And what would you want that meeting to know about you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"That I've cut down. That I go to every midwife appointment. That I'm not my mum."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"Those things matter, and I'll make sure they're heard.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:12","band":"Explain the process","w":"D","s":"Can I explain how it works? The social worker is doing a pre-birth assessment: what's going well, what worries people, and what help you need.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"The conference brings together you, the social worker, your midwife, me and others. You can bring someone, like Jade, your personal adviser. You'll see the reports before the day.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"I didn't know I could see them."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"You can. And you'll see mine before I send it.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"If I do everything you say, will I keep her?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I wish I could promise that. I can't, and I won't lie to you. The meeting could decide on no plan, on support as a child in need, or a child protection plan.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"If they're very worried, they could look at a plan for after the birth, like a foster placement where you and the baby live together. If that happens, you'd get a solicitor.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"So they could take her. I knew it."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"It's possible, and I know that's terrifying. It's not decided. What you do in the next three weeks really counts.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"I want to cut right now. I can't do this."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me rather than hiding it. Let's stay with this for a minute. What helped last time the urge was this strong?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Ice. Ringing Jade."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"Shall we ring Jade together after we finish here? And there's a cold water bottle by the sink; hold it for a bit.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"OK. Yeah."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Risks","w":"D","s":"When did you last hurt yourself?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Two months ago. Just my arm. I get urges most weeks."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Have you had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Sometimes, when it's bad. No plan. She stops me."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"She's a strong reason. Tell me about drugs and drink now: cannabis, cocaine, alcohol, cigarettes.","m":[7,6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Couple of joints at night. Coke, three weeks ago at a party. No drink. Ten fags."},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"That's a real drop from all day. That's going in my report as a strength.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"4:01","band":"Domestic abuse","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this in pregnancy. Has Ryan ever hurt you, frightened you or controlled what you do?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"He pushed me once. Into a wall. He was stressed. And he checks my phone."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that was brave. Being pushed isn't OK, whatever the stress. Can I ask a few more questions, so we can keep you and the baby safe?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"Fine. But don't make it worse."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"I'll go at your pace. I'd like to refer you to an IDVA, an advocate who helps with safety. Is that OK?","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"Yeah, OK."},{"t":"4:51","band":"Sharing and the report","w":"D","s":"I do need to tell the social worker about Ryan, because it affects your safety and the baby's. I'll tell you exactly what I say, and you can help me word it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Will they hold it against me?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Being hurt by someone isn't your fault. Telling us, and accepting help, counts in your favour.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"My report will say your mental health history, the self-harm and drugs, and Ryan. It'll also say you've cut down, you've been to every appointment, and you're honest with me.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:33","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Three things you control before the conference. See the substance misuse midwife next week and do weekly drug tests.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Join the skills group with our psychologist on Thursdays, for managing urges. And meet the IDVA.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"I can do that. I'm still not sure about going to the conference."},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"If you're there, they hear your side from you. Jade can come with you. Would that help?","m":[3]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If Jade comes."},{"t":"6:11","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"You'll show me the report. Drug tests every week. The group on Thursdays. Some advocate for Ryan stuff. And ring Jade now."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If things get bad, ring us in the day, or 111, mental health option, at night. 999 if you're in danger. I'll see you next Friday.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"You actually told me the truth. Nobody does that."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll keep doing it. Let's ring Jade.","m":[12]}]}},
{"id":"c-p-12","n":1012,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Severe anxiety and panic at 28 weeks, no medicines wanted: assessment and a therapy-first plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 09:30","where":"Perinatal mental health outpatient clinic","text":"Ms Mei Chen, 27, is 28 weeks pregnant with her first baby. Her midwife refers her with six weeks of panic attacks, three or four a week, and constant worry that something is wrong with the baby. She has stopped taking the bus and shopping alone. Her blood pressure and urine were normal last week. She writes on the referral form: 'I do not want any medication.'"},"task":"Assess her anxiety and panic, rule out physical causes, assess risk, and agree a plan led by psychological therapy that respects her choice.","first60":["Read the midwife's notes: BP, urine, booking bloods, Hb, scans and any obstetric concerns.","Check the referral for previous anxiety and what helped.","Note her wish for no medicines; plan to respect it from the first minute.","Have the local NHS Talking Therapies perinatal pathway details and a panic self-help leaflet ready.","Prepare GAD-7 and a quick panic history."],"ask":["'Tell me what happens in your body and your mind during an attack.'","'How long do they last? Do they come out of the blue, or in certain places?'","'What do you worry about most of the day?'","'Have you had breathlessness at rest, chest pain, a swollen leg or palpitations outside the attacks?'","'What are you avoiding because of the panic?'","'How do you feel about the birth itself?'","'Has your mood been low? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?'","'What helped last time you felt anxious?'"],"recognise":["Panic disorder: recurrent unexpected attacks with palpitations, breathlessness, dizziness and fear of dying or losing control, then worry about further attacks and avoidance.","Generalised anxiety: persistent hard-to-control worry, here focused on the baby; check for fear of childbirth (tokophobia) and obsessive checking.","Rule out physical causes in pregnancy: anaemia, thyroid disease, arrhythmia, and pulmonary embolism (breathlessness at rest, pleuritic pain, calf swelling, low sats); also pre-eclampsia (headache, visual change, high BP).","Anxiety in pregnancy is common and is a strong predictor of postnatal depression and anxiety.","Psychological therapy (CBT for panic) is the first-line treatment and works well in pregnancy.","Her decision about medicines is informed if she knows the options and risks either way; respect it."],"decide":[{"c":"Confirm panic disorder with anxiety about the baby, check physical causes, assess risk, refer for high-intensity CBT on the perinatal priority pathway, and agree self-help and review, leaving medicine as an option she can revisit.","r":"Correct: NICE recommends psychological therapy first for anxiety disorders, and this respects her choice while keeping her safe.","best":true},{"c":"Persuade her to start sertraline today because it is the quickest fix.","r":"Wrong: therapy is first line, she has made a capacitous choice, and pressure damages trust.","best":false},{"c":"Prescribe diazepam 2 mg as needed for attacks.","r":"Unsafe: benzodiazepines are not recommended for anxiety in pregnancy except very short-term in severe situations; they risk dependence and neonatal problems.","best":false}],"law":["NICE CG192: for a woman with an anxiety disorder in pregnancy, offer a high-intensity psychological intervention; she should be assessed within 2 weeks of referral and start therapy within 1 month of initial assessment.","NICE CG192: do not offer benzodiazepines to women in pregnancy except for short-term treatment of severe anxiety and agitation.","She has capacity to refuse medication; an informed refusal is valid (MCA 2005). Record the discussion.","If fear of childbirth is significant, NICE CG192 and NG192 support referral for specialist perinatal support and a discussion of birth options with the obstetric team."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"What you're describing sounds like panic attacks, with a lot of worry about the baby. They're very frightening, but they're not dangerous to you or the baby, and they're very treatable."},{"to":"Patient (medicines)","s":"I hear that you don't want medication, and that's completely your choice. The best first treatment is a talking therapy anyway, and that's what I'd recommend."},{"to":"Patient (keeping options open)","s":"If the therapy isn't enough, we can talk about medicine again, with the facts in front of you. You'd always decide."}],"write":"09:30 Perinatal clinic. Mei Chen 27F, 28/40, G1. Midwife referral.\nHx: 6/52 panic attacks 3-4/week, peak 10 min: palpitations, breathlessness, tingling, fear of dying. Avoids buses and shopping alone. Daily worry about the baby; checks movements many times a day. Fear of labour after friend's traumatic birth. Mood: anxious, not depressed; sleep 6 h. Anxiety at university, CBT helped. No alcohol or drugs. Caffeine 4 cups/day.\nPhysical: no breathlessness at rest, no chest pain, no calf swelling. BP 116/72 and urine NAD (midwife 1/52). HR 84 reg, sats 99%.\nRisk: denies self-harm or suicidal thoughts; no thoughts of harming baby. Partner supportive.\nGAD-7 15.\nA: Panic disorder with agoraphobic avoidance and pregnancy-focused anxiety. Fear of childbirth.\nP: Informed choice: no medication. Referral to NHS Talking Therapies perinatal pathway for high-intensity CBT for panic. Self-help: panic leaflet, slow breathing, reduce caffeine. Bloods: FBC, ferritin, TFTs. Midwife: birth plan conversation; MMHS referral if fear of childbirth persists. Review 2/52.\nDr S. Rahman CT2","make":["Ask her to walk you through one recent attack, from trigger to end.","Ask the physical red-flag questions and check pulse and sats yourself.","Ask about avoidance, worry about the baby, checking and fear of birth.","Ask about mood, self-harm and thoughts of harm to the baby.","Acknowledge her no-medicines decision early, so she can listen.","Explain panic with the cycle: sensation, fear, more sensation; then agree therapy, self-help and review."],"next":"Mei starts CBT for panic four weeks later. Meanwhile she uses the breathing technique, cuts to one coffee a day, and takes the bus with her partner twice a week. Her FBC shows Hb 102 g/L and the midwife starts oral iron. By 36 weeks attacks are down to one a week, and she writes a birth plan with her midwife.","marks":["Took a clear panic history and screened for worry, avoidance and fear of birth.","Excluded physical causes with targeted questions and checks.","Assessed mood, self-harm and thoughts of harm to the baby.","Respected her decision about medicines from the start.","Explained panic simply and reassured about the baby honestly.","Agreed therapy, self-help, bloods and review with dates."],"pitfalls":["Pushing medication against her stated wish.","Labelling breathlessness as panic without asking about PE.","Prescribing a benzodiazepine.","Forgetting that anxiety now raises the risk of postnatal illness."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG113 GAD and panic guideline (2011, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. RCOG Green-top Guideline 37b: thromboembolic disease in pregnancy (2015; checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: anxiety in pregnancy; panic disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients expect to choose. A woman who declines medicines is not 'non-compliant'. Give her the facts, respect her decision and make therapy happen quickly.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"History to a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Diagnose her panic and anxiety, exclude physical causes and risk, and agree a CBT-first plan without medication.","obj":["Took a panic history and screened for worry, avoidance, checking and fear of childbirth","Asked about breathlessness at rest, chest pain, calf swelling and palpitations, and arranged FBC, ferritin and TFTs","Assessed mood, self-harm and thoughts of harm to the baby","Explained panic in plain words and reassured her honestly that attacks do not harm the baby","Agreed high-intensity CBT, self-help and a 2-week review, respecting her choice not to take medicine"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Every time it happens I think I'm going to die and the baby will die with me.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. Panic makes your body feel in danger when it isn't. Your heart and breathing speed up, but attacks pass and don't hurt you or the baby.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want any tablets. I've read they cause problems for the baby.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and that's your choice. The best first treatment is talking therapy anyway. If you ever want to talk about medicines, I'll give you the real facts, and you decide.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do you know it's not my heart or something with the pregnancy?\"","r":"\"Good question. Let me ask some specific things and check your pulse and oxygen. I'll also do blood tests for anaemia and thyroid, which can cause similar feelings.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I keep thinking the stress will damage the baby.\"","r":"\"Many mums-to-be worry about that. Everyday stress and panic attacks don't damage the baby. Getting help now is good for both of you, and you're doing that.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't even get on a bus any more. I feel pathetic.\"","r":"\"You're not pathetic. Avoiding is what everyone does with panic, and it keeps the fear going. Therapy helps you take those steps back, a little at a time.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'm terrified of the birth. My friend nearly died.\"","r":"\"No wonder you're frightened, after seeing that. Let's make sure your midwife knows. You can talk through birth options with the team, and there's a specialist service if the fear stays strong.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication: informed choice recorded. If she later chooses one, sertraline 50 mg once daily is commonly used in pregnancy; avoid benzodiazepines","Bloods: FBC, ferritin, TFTs; ECG if palpitations occur outside panic or are irregular","Cut caffeine to one cup a day (pregnancy limit 200 mg/day)","Red flags for same-day review: breathlessness at rest, chest pain, calf swelling, severe headache or visual change"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT for panic disorder via NHS Talking Therapies perinatal pathway (priority), about 12 to 14 weekly sessions","While waiting: NHS panic self-help, slow breathing (in 4, out 6), and planned graded steps back onto the bus with her partner","Referral to the maternal mental health service if fear of childbirth stays severe after initial therapy"],"social":["Midwife: early birth-plan conversation and options discussion with the obstetric team if fear persists","Partner invited to a session so he can help with graded exposure and know the plan","Health visitor told before birth: higher risk of postnatal anxiety or depression"],"safety":["Review in 2 weeks in perinatal clinic; GAD-7 at each contact","If mood drops or thoughts of self-harm appear: contact the perinatal team in hours, 111 mental health option out of hours","Postnatal plan in her notes: perinatal team review within 2 weeks of birth"]},"facts":[{"h":"Therapy first for anxiety","x":"NICE recommends high-intensity psychological therapy for anxiety disorders in pregnancy: assessment within 2 weeks of referral, therapy within 1 month of assessment."},{"h":"No benzodiazepines in pregnancy","x":"NICE: only short-term for severe anxiety and agitation. Risks include dependence and neonatal sedation or withdrawal if used near birth."},{"h":"Think PE before panic","x":"Pregnancy raises clot risk. Breathlessness at rest, pleuritic pain, a swollen calf or low sats need same-day medical assessment, not reassurance."},{"h":"Panic does not harm the baby","x":"Attacks are frightening but short and self-limiting. Saying this clearly and honestly lowers the fear that fuels the next attack."},{"h":"Anxiety predicts postnatal illness","x":"Antenatal anxiety raises the risk of postnatal depression and anxiety. Write a postnatal plan and tell the health visitor."}],"sim":{"setup":"Wednesday 09:30, perinatal clinic. Mei Chen, 27, is 28 weeks pregnant with panic attacks and constant worry about the baby. She has written that she wants no medication. Assess her and agree a plan led by psychological therapy.","role":{"who":"Ms Mei Chen, 27, 28 weeks pregnant with her first baby","open":"Before we start, I've written it on the form. I don't want any tablets. Please don't push me.","manner":"Tense, articulate and guarded about medicines; relaxes visibly once her choice is respected. Becomes tearful when describing fear of the baby dying in an attack; calms with clear explanation.","free":["Panic attacks three or four times a week for six weeks","She worries all day that something is wrong with the baby","She has stopped taking the bus and shopping alone","She does not want medicines because she has read they harm babies"],"ifasked":["Attacks: heart pounding, breathless, tingling hands, sure she will die; peak in about ten minutes","No breathlessness at rest, no chest pain, no swollen leg; palpitations only during attacks","She checks the baby's movements many times a day","She is terrified of labour since a friend had a traumatic birth","Had anxiety at university; CBT helped","Mood not low; sleeping six hours; no thoughts of self-harm or of harming the baby","Drinks four cups of coffee a day"],"worries":["Will the panic hurt the baby?","How do you know it's not my heart?","How long will I wait for therapy?"],"rules":["Stays guarded until the doctor explicitly accepts her no-medicines choice","If medication is pushed, she says she will not come back","Becomes tearful when describing thinking the baby will die with her; settles when reassured with facts","Mentions fear of childbirth only if asked how she feels about the birth"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and accepts her choice about medicines early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a panic history: symptoms, duration, frequency and triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about worry about the baby, checking, avoidance and fear of childbirth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks physical red-flag questions for PE and cardiac causes and checks pulse and sats","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past anxiety and what helped, and about caffeine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses mood, self-harm and thoughts of harm to the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains panic in plain words and reassures honestly that it does not harm the baby","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends CBT as first-line therapy and explains timescales","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees self-help, caffeine reduction and blood tests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Involves the midwife about fear of birth and keeps medicine as an option she can revisit","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives safety netting, including physical red flags, and a review date","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to summarise","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Respects her choice from the start, makes a confident diagnosis, excludes physical causes and risk, and agrees a clear therapy-led plan.","borderline":"Good plan but physical causes or fear of childbirth missed, or her choice acknowledged late.","fail":"Pushes medication or prescribes a benzodiazepine, or attributes breathlessness to panic without asking about PE."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mei. I'm Dr Rahman from the perinatal team. Your midwife asked me to see you about the panic attacks.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Before we start, I've written it on the form. I don't want any tablets. Please don't push me."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me straight away. I won't push you. It's your choice, and the best first treatment for this is a talking therapy anyway.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Oh. OK. Good."},{"t":"0:38","band":"The attacks","w":"D","s":"Could you walk me through the last attack, from the start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I was in the supermarket. My heart started pounding. I couldn't breathe, my hands tingled. I was sure I was going to die."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"How long did it last, and how often do they happen?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"It's worst after about ten minutes, then it fades. Three or four a week for six weeks."},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"And every time I think the baby will die with me."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, to think that every time. Thank you for telling me.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:29","band":"Worry and avoidance","w":"D","s":"Between attacks, what do you worry about?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"The baby. All day. I check the movements constantly."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Are there places you've stopped going?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"The bus. Shopping on my own. I feel pathetic."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"You're not pathetic. Avoiding is what everyone does with panic. The trouble is that it keeps the fear going.","m":[7,3]},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about the birth itself?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Terrified. My friend had an awful birth last year. She nearly died."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"No wonder you're frightened after seeing that. I'll make sure we address it.","m":[7,3]},{"t":"2:22","band":"Physical causes","w":"P","s":"How do you know it's not my heart? Or the pregnancy?"},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"Good question. Pregnancy changes the body, so I need to check. Do you get breathless when you're resting, or have chest pain, or a swollen, painful leg?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"No. Only during the attacks."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"And palpitations outside the attacks?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"I'll check your pulse and oxygen now, and I'd like blood tests for anaemia and thyroid, which can cause similar feelings.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Your pulse is 84 and regular, and your oxygen is 99 percent. That's reassuring.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:14","band":"Background and risk","w":"D","s":"Have you felt like this before?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"At university. I had CBT. It really helped."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"That's really useful to know. How much coffee or tea do you drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Four coffees a day. I'm so tired."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any times you've felt so low you've had thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"No. I'm anxious, not sad. I'd never hurt myself."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"And any thoughts of harming the baby, even frightening ones?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"No. I just want her to be safe."},{"t":"4:04","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Mei, this sounds like panic disorder, with a lot of worry about the baby. In panic, your body sounds an alarm when there's no danger. Your heart races, you breathe fast, and that feels scary, so the alarm gets louder.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Attacks are frightening, but they pass and they don't harm you or the baby. Everyday stress doesn't damage her either.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Really? I've been so scared it was hurting her."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Really. And getting help now is good for both of you.","m":[8,7]},{"t":"4:49","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"CBT for panic is the first-line treatment, and it worked for you before. I'll refer you to NHS Talking Therapies on the pregnancy priority pathway. You should be assessed within a couple of weeks.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"And until then?"},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"A slow breathing technique: in for four, out for six. A self-help guide for panic. And cutting coffee to one a day, which will help the jitteriness.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"Taking the bus with your partner, short trips first, is a good start too.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"I'll let your midwife know about your fear of the birth, so you can talk through your options. There's a specialist service if the fear stays strong.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"D","s":"If the therapy isn't enough, we can talk about medicine again, with honest facts. You'd always decide.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"That feels fair."},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If you get breathless at rest, chest pain, a swollen leg, or a bad headache with blurred vision, get seen the same day. That isn't panic.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"It's panic, not my heart. It won't hurt the baby. CBT referral, breathing, one coffee, blood tests. Tell the midwife about the birth. No tablets."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Exactly that. I'll see you in two weeks. Anything else on your mind?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"No. I feel lighter. Thank you for listening."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You've done the hard part by coming. See you in two weeks.","m":[12]}]}}
);
